window.QD={"src": ["Comp 1 Practice (V1)", "Comp 3 Practice", "Comp Bible Part 3", "Comp Bible Pt 3 – Set 2 & Exam 1 Recording", "Class slides – Physiological Adaptation", "Class slides – Safety & Infection Control", "Class slides – Pharmacology & Parenteral", "Class slides – Management of Care", "Class slides – Psychosocial Integrity", "Class slides – Reduction of Risk Potential", "Class slides – OB & Newborn", "Class slides – Health Promotion", "Class slides – Basic Care & Comfort", "Class slides – NCLEX Test-Taking", "Class slides – Pediatrics", "Class slides – Respiratory", "Class slides – GI", "Class slides – Cardiac", "Class slides – Hematology", "Class slides – Endocrine", "Class slides – Neurological", "Class slides – Musculoskeletal"], "top": ["Prioritization & Delegation", "Infection Control & Safety", "Legal, Ethics & Management", "Cardiovascular", "Respiratory", "Renal, Fluids & Acid-Base", "Endocrine & Metabolic", "GI & Nutrition", "Neuro, Eyes & Ortho", "Burns, Trauma & Emergencies", "Pharmacology & Labs", "Maternal-Newborn", "Pediatrics", "Mental Health", "Health Promotion & Prevention", "Oncology & Radiation", "Skills & Procedures"], "bank": [{"id": 1, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse on a medical-surgical unit has been made aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first plan to assess the client who", "o": ["has type 2 diabetes mellitus with a premeal glucose of 85 mg/dL and a postmeal glucose of 220 mg/dL.", "is 24 hours postoperative from abdominal surgery and wound drainage has changed from sanguineous to serosanguineous over 2 hours.", "is preparing for a colonoscopy and whose BP was 128/74 mm Hg and is now 106/60 mm Hg.", "has acute pancreatitis and reports an unchanged pain level of 7/10, 30 minutes after an oral pain medication."], "a": 2, "r": "Priority = unstable/changing physiology first. A falling BP during bowel prep suggests fluid volume deficit (hypovolemia) and is the only true change that threatens circulation. Sanguineous→serosanguineous drainage is the expected healing progression, and a postmeal glucose of 220 is an expected rise. Unchanged pain is a concern, but it is not as acutely unstable as dropping BP."}, {"id": 2, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse has received hand-off report and is assigning tasks to unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP). Which task should the nurse instruct the UAP to perform first?", "o": ["Obtain a sputum specimen from a client with suspected pulmonary TB.", "Obtain a capillary blood glucose on a client who had a hypoglycemic episode 30 minutes ago and received IV dextrose.", "Turn and reposition a client who was last turned 2 hours ago.", "Deliver a lunch tray to a client returning from radiology who has been NPO since breakfast."], "a": 2, "r": "Delegation: UAP perform routine tasks on STABLE clients. A client who just had hypoglycemia and IV dextrose is unstable; the RN must monitor and interpret that glucose. Of the remaining tasks, turning is time-critical now (q2h to prevent pressure injury). The sputum specimen is routine, and the NPO client should be cleared by the RN before eating."}, {"id": 3, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse on a pediatric unit is reviewing client data. Which client should the nurse plan to see first?", "o": ["The infant with pertussis receiving oxygen via nasal cannula.", "The adolescent with sickle cell crisis who is being discharged.", "The toddler with bilateral arm casts who needs breakfast fed to them.", "The school-aged child with type 1 diabetes who requires blood glucose monitoring."], "a": 0, "r": "ABCs: an infant with pertussis has a high risk of apnea and airway obstruction and is already requiring oxygen. The other three are stable, routine needs."}, {"id": 4, "t": 2, "s": 0, "q": "A newly hired nurse is discussing fiscally responsible practice with the preceptor. Which statement requires follow-up?", "o": ["“Involving the family in client care activities is an example of fiscal responsibility.”", "“Engaging in safe clinical practice to avoid personal injuries is an example of fiscal responsibility.”", "“Completing assigned nursing care during scheduled hours is a way to demonstrate fiscal responsibility.”", "“Following evidence-based best practice guidelines demonstrates fiscal responsibility.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Fiscal responsibility means using resources wisely: avoiding injury and errors, completing work within scheduled hours (no overtime), and following EBP to prevent costly complications. Involving family is family-centered care and does not directly relate to cost, so it is the statement that needs follow-up."}, {"id": 5, "t": 2, "s": 0, "q": "The preceptor observes a newly hired nurse teaching a client with pelvic inflammatory disease who does not speak English. Which action requires intervention?", "o": ["Contacting the charge nurse to obtain a facility-approved interpreter.", "Providing written instructions in the client’s primary language.", "Using a facility-approved telephone interpretation service.", "Having a family member who speaks both languages interpret the instructions."], "a": 3, "r": "Use a qualified, facility-approved interpreter. Family members may alter or omit information, and the topic is sensitive (confidentiality/HIPAA). Written materials in the client’s language and phone interpretation are acceptable."}, {"id": 6, "t": 11, "s": 0, "q": "A client in labor has just had spontaneous rupture of membranes. Which finding is a priority to report to the PHCP?", "o": ["Contractions lasting 60 seconds.", "New appearance of late decelerations.", "Maternal temperature of 99.5° F.", "Four contractions in a 10-minute period."], "a": 1, "r": "Late decelerations indicate uteroplacental insufficiency (fetal hypoxia). After rupture, cord prolapse/placental problems are also a concern. The other findings are normal for labor (contractions ≤5 in 10 min, 60 s duration, T <100.4° F)."}, {"id": 7, "t": 16, "s": 0, "q": "The preceptor observes a newly hired nurse caring for clients. Which action indicates correct care?", "o": ["Placing an N95 on a client with active TB before transport to a long-term care facility.", "Administering oral pain medication to a client 30 minutes before discharge.", "Using a 23-gauge, 1-inch needle for an IM injection in the deltoid.", "Teaching a client with a new ileostomy to empty the bag 30 minutes after eating."], "a": 2, "r": "IM injection: 22–25 gauge, 1–1.5 in (adult deltoid) is appropriate. The CLIENT with TB wears a surgical mask (the nurse wears the N95). Ostomy pouches are emptied when 1/3 full and not right after meals, when output peaks."}, {"id": 8, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "In an outpatient clinic, which client should the nurse have the PHCP see first?", "o": ["15-year-old with a bull’s-eye rash 2 days after a camping trip.", "37-year-old with polycystic kidney disease and lumbar pain when ambulating.", "45-year-old with pleurisy and worsening shortness of breath.", "57-year-old with Graves’ disease who has heat intolerance and diaphoresis."], "a": 2, "r": "Worsening shortness of breath is an airway/breathing (ABC) problem. Heat intolerance in Graves’ disease, flank pain with PKD, and an early Lyme rash are expected or less urgent."}, {"id": 9, "t": 15, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first plan to see the client who has", "o": ["cervical cancer, receives internal radiation therapy, and whose partner has been at the bedside for 2 hours.", "neutropenia (WBC 3,000/mm³) and is eating homemade cookies from a visitor.", "bone marrow suppression, receiving epoetin alfa, with BP changing from 140/94 to 124/80.", "thrombocytopenia and a family member has brought fresh fruit and flowers."], "a": 0, "r": "Internal (sealed) radiation: visitors are limited (about 30 minutes/day) and must stay ~6 ft from the source. A partner at the bedside for 2 hours is being unsafely exposed, so the nurse intervenes. The other situations are not immediate hazards for those diagnoses."}, {"id": 10, "t": 9, "s": 0, "q": "A male client has full-thickness burns to 50% of the lower body. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has a", "o": ["hematocrit of 60%.", "SpO₂ of 91%.", "urinary output of 100 mL over the past 2 hours.", "temperature of 104.2° F."], "a": 3, "r": "A temperature of 104.2° F suggests sepsis/infection, the leading cause of death in burn clients. Hemoconcentration (high Hct) is an expected early finding, and 50 mL/hr urine output is adequate (≥30 mL/hr)."}, {"id": 11, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "Following an explosion at a local factory, the ED nurse triages clients. Which client is the priority for treatment?", "o": ["Closed tibia fracture with severe pain.", "Type 1 diabetes who feels shaky with clammy skin.", "Large head wound with exposed gray matter and RR of 6.", "Second-degree burns to chest and arms with RR greater than 30."], "a": 3, "r": "Triage: treat life-threatening but salvageable problems first. Burns of the chest with tachypnea suggest airway/inhalation injury. The head wound with exposed brain and RR 6 is likely expectant (unsurvivable)."}, {"id": 12, "t": 5, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with severe hypocalcemia. Which should be included in the plan of care?", "o": ["Encourage foods high in phosphorus.", "Measure urine output every 2 hours.", "Place the client on seizure precautions.", "Encourage increased fluid intake."], "a": 2, "r": "Low calcium increases neuromuscular excitability: tetany, laryngospasm, seizures. Phosphorus is inversely related to calcium and would worsen the imbalance."}, {"id": 13, "t": 1, "s": 0, "q": "Which action by the nurse is appropriate?", "o": ["Placing a client who had a stem cell transplant in contact isolation.", "Having a client with pertussis wear a surgical mask during transport.", "Initiating airborne precautions for a client with epiglottitis.", "Wearing an N95 when caring for a client with impetigo."], "a": 1, "r": "Pertussis = droplet precautions; the client wears a surgical mask when transported. A transplant recipient needs protective (reverse) isolation, epiglottitis is droplet, and impetigo is contact."}, {"id": 14, "t": 1, "s": 0, "q": "In an ambulatory center, the nurse should immediately remove from the waiting room and place in an exam room the client who has", "o": ["heart failure and a 3 lb weight gain in 3 days.", "a fever of 101.8° F, headache, vomiting, and photophobia.", "myasthenia gravis, left ptosis, and a nasal-sounding voice.", "a 1 mm flat, red area at the site of a Mantoux test given 48 hours ago."], "a": 1, "r": "Fever, headache, vomiting and photophobia suggest meningitis (droplet precautions), so protect others and expedite care. A 1 mm area is a negative PPD; the HF and MG clients need follow-up but are not infectious."}, {"id": 15, "t": 15, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with a sealed radiation implant. Which precaution should the nurse implement?", "o": ["Limit each visitor to 1 hour per day.", "Keep dedicated equipment in the client’s room until the implant is removed.", "Assign a different nurse to care for the client each day.", "Place bed linens in a biohazard receptacle outside the client’s room."], "a": 2, "r": "Limit staff exposure by rotating nurses (time, distance, shielding). Visitors should be limited to about 30 minutes/day, and linens stay in the room until the implant is removed. Source note: dedicated equipment is listed as correct on other versions of this question, so check your instructor’s key."}, {"id": 16, "t": 1, "s": 0, "q": "A fire with smoke is noted in the nurses’ station. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Move clients close to a window with unit doors closed.", "Use a bag valve mask to move a client on a mechanical ventilator.", "Transfer clients by elevator to another floor.", "Extinguish the fire starting at the ceiling and moving down."], "a": 1, "r": "RACE: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish. Ventilator-dependent clients are evacuated while manually ventilated. Never use elevators in a fire, and extinguish by aiming at the BASE of the fire (PASS)."}, {"id": 17, "t": 14, "s": 0, "q": "Which action by the nurse is an example of PRIMARY prevention?", "o": ["Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes.", "Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group.", "Recommending helmets when riding bicycles or motorcycles.", "Teaching monthly breast self-examination."], "a": 2, "r": "Primary = prevent disease/injury before it occurs (vaccines, helmets, healthy diet). Screening/BSE is secondary; foot care and support groups limit progression/complications (tertiary)."}, {"id": 18, "t": 10, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is about to give scheduled immunizations to an immunocompromised child. Which should the nurse question?", "o": ["Haemophilus influenzae type B.", "Inactivated poliovirus.", "Hepatitis B.", "Live attenuated influenza."], "a": 3, "r": "Live vaccines (LAIV, MMR, varicella, rotavirus) are contraindicated in immunocompromised clients. Inactivated vaccines are safe."}, {"id": 19, "t": 12, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is teaching the mother of a 9-year-old child about age-appropriate toys. Which is appropriate?", "o": ["Finger paints and bubbles.", "Board games and reading books.", "Large building blocks and balls.", "Wooden puzzles."], "a": 1, "r": "School-age (6–12) children are industrious: cooperative play, rules, board games, collections, reading, and sports. The other toys suit toddlers and preschoolers."}, {"id": 20, "t": 0, "s": 0, "q": "At a health fair, for which client is it a priority to recommend follow-up with a PHCP?", "o": ["65 y/o with elevated HDL, decreased LDL, and who does not follow a low-cholesterol diet.", "55 y/o with BP 164/96 mm Hg who stopped smoking 2 months ago.", "50 y/o with a family history of ovarian cancer who has not had the HPV vaccine.", "45 y/o with exercise-induced asthma and vesicular breath sounds in the peripheral lung fields."], "a": 1, "r": "BP 164/96 is stage 2 hypertension and needs evaluation. High HDL and low LDL are desirable, and vesicular peripheral breath sounds are normal."}, {"id": 21, "t": 12, "s": 0, "q": "In a well-baby clinic, it is most important to suggest follow-up for the infant who is", "o": ["6 months old and unable to roll over.", "4 months old and the posterior fontanel is closed.", "3 months old and unable to switch objects between hands.", "4 months old and sleeps up to 9 hours at night."], "a": 0, "r": "By ~6 months infants roll both ways, so this is a gross-motor delay. The posterior fontanel closes at 2–3 months, hand-to-hand transfer comes around 6 months, and 9-hour sleep is normal."}, {"id": 22, "t": 13, "s": 0, "q": "The parent of a child with leukemia is sitting at the bedside crying and says, “I’m so worried about my child.” Which response is most therapeutic?", "o": ["“I’ll stay here and sit with you awhile.”", "“Are you able to take good care of yourself?”", "“Have you considered attending a support group?”", "“Life must seem very scary right now.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Offering presence is the most therapeutic response for a distressed parent. Other options are closed-ended, give advice prematurely, or put words in the parent’s mouth."}, {"id": 23, "t": 13, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is planning care for a client with histrionic personality disorder. Which intervention is appropriate?", "o": ["Work with the client on ways to develop trusting relationships.", "Communicate with the client using concrete and descriptive language.", "Have the client remain quiet and observe during group therapy.", "Use compassionate behavior to keep the client in control."], "a": 1, "r": "Cluster B/histrionic clients are dramatic and vague. Use concrete, direct, descriptive communication and set clear limits."}, {"id": 24, "t": 13, "s": 0, "q": "On a mental health unit, which client should the nurse assess first?", "o": ["Bipolar disorder, manic, no sleep in 24 hours, pacing the hallways.", "Major depressive disorder overheard saying, “I can’t take it anymore.”", "Suicidal ideation, reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with staff.", "Anorexia nervosa who went to the restroom 15 minutes after a meal."], "a": 1, "r": "Safety first. A statement like “I can’t take it anymore” may signal suicidal intent, and no one has assessed it yet. Review sheet: assess the client with depression who says “I can’t do this anymore” first."}, {"id": 25, "t": 12, "s": 0, "q": "A parent of a 3-year-old says the child is in constant motion and cannot sit through a story, and asks if the child is hyperactive. Which response is most appropriate?", "o": ["“It is normal for parents to worry about hyperactivity.”", "“I am not seeing any obvious signs of hyperactivity.”", "“At 3 years of age, it is hard to tell.”", "“Describe when and where this occurs.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Use therapeutic communication: open-ended exploration to gather data. Reassurance and dismissive responses block communication."}, {"id": 26, "t": 8, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke. Which statement indicates a need for additional teaching?", "o": ["“I will make sure my partner has a snack 1 to 2 hours before going to bed.”", "“I should have my partner alternate a bite of food with sips of water.”", "“A change in my partner’s voice after swallowing may indicate dysphagia.”", "“Hesitancy to eat could indicate difficulty swallowing.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Aspiration risk: with dysphagia, avoid eating close to bedtime and stay upright after meals. Wet or gurgly voice after swallowing and reluctance to eat are signs of dysphagia. (Key per source.)"}, {"id": 27, "t": 7, "s": 0, "q": "A client with GERD has been taught diet and lifestyle changes. Which statement indicates a need for additional teaching?", "o": ["“I will sit upright for 1 to 2 hours after meals.”", "“I will have a snack 1 hour before bedtime.”", "“I will avoid tight clothing.”", "“I will sleep with the head of my bed elevated 6 to 12 inches.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Do not eat within 2–3 hours of lying down. Eating before bed increases reflux. The other statements are correct."}, {"id": 28, "t": 16, "s": 0, "q": "A newly hired nurse is caring for a client who died of pneumonia. Which action requires intervention by the charge nurse?", "o": ["Attaching 2 ID tags to the body and 1 on the outside of the shroud.", "Leaving drainage tubes and IV lines in the body prior to autopsy.", "Leaving any jewelry on the client to be transported to the funeral home.", "Asking the family if they wish to participate in bathing the body."], "a": 2, "r": "Remove jewelry and give it to the family, documenting the transfer (a wedding band may be left only per policy). Tubes/lines are left in if an autopsy is expected, and family participation is encouraged."}, {"id": 29, "t": 3, "s": 0, "q": "The client has a new order for graduated compression stockings. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Measure the size of the client’s foot.", "Apply the stockings while the client is sitting in a chair.", "Check the stockings for wrinkles or creases.", "Remove the stockings once each day for 10 minutes."], "a": 2, "r": "Wrinkles act like a tourniquet and impair circulation. Measure the LEG, apply before rising (supine, legs elevated), and remove for ~30 minutes at least every 8–24 hours per policy for skin care and assessment."}, {"id": 30, "t": 16, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is preparing a Mantoux test. Which action indicates correct technique?", "o": ["Injecting in the subcutaneous tissue.", "Drawing up 0.1 mL of PPD tuberculin.", "Using a 20-gauge needle.", "Inserting the needle at a 90-degree angle."], "a": 1, "r": "Mantoux = intradermal injection of 0.1 mL PPD with a 25–27 gauge, ⅜–½ inch needle at 5–15° (bevel up) to raise a wheal."}, {"id": 31, "t": 10, "s": 0, "q": "A client is receiving O positive packed red blood cells. The client’s blood type is B positive. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Administer acetaminophen and diphenhydramine PRN.", "Immediately stop the transfusion.", "Document on an incident report.", "Continue to assess the client."], "a": 3, "r": "O-negative is the universal donor, and O blood is ABO-compatible with a B-positive recipient. The Rh factor matches, so the transfusion is compatible. Continue to monitor for reactions."}, {"id": 32, "t": 10, "s": 0, "q": "A PHCP prescribes vaccines for an older adult. Which should the nurse clarify?", "o": ["Inactivated polio (IPV) series.", "Influenza (quadrivalent).", "Herpes zoster (Zostavax).", "Tetanus, diphtheria, pertussis (Tdap) booster."], "a": 0, "r": "Older adults routinely receive influenza, pneumococcal, Td/Tdap and zoster. Polio vaccine is not routinely given to adults unless at risk or unvaccinated, so clarify it. (Zostavax was a live vaccine that has since been replaced by the recombinant Shingrix.)"}, {"id": 33, "t": 10, "s": 0, "q": "A client is prescribed warfarin for a prosthetic heart valve. Which finding indicates a therapeutic effect?", "o": ["PT of 11.6 seconds.", "PTT of 20 seconds.", "aPTT of 35 seconds.", "INR of 3."], "a": 3, "r": "Therapeutic INR on warfarin is 2–3 (2.5–3.5 for mechanical valves). A PT of 11.6 s is normal (not anticoagulated); PTT/aPTT monitor heparin."}, {"id": 34, "t": 9, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse starts LR for a burn client prescribed 5,200 mL over the first 24 hours (half in the first 8 hours). How many mL/hr for the first 8 hours?", "o": ["350 mL/hr", "433 mL/hr", "325 mL/hr", "217 mL/hr"], "a": 2, "r": "Half of 5,200 = 2,600 mL in 8 hours → 2,600 ÷ 8 = 325 mL/hr."}, {"id": 35, "t": 16, "s": 0, "q": "Which action is appropriate when preparing to administer insulin to an adult?", "o": ["Limit the dose to 1 mL when injecting into the deltoid muscle.", "Insert the needle at a 15-degree angle.", "Draw up the dose using a 25-gauge, ⅜-inch needle.", "Administer the dose using the Z-track method."], "a": 2, "r": "Insulin is subcutaneous: 25–30 gauge, ⅜–⅝ inch needle at 45–90°. Z-track and deltoid volume limits are IM techniques; 15° is intradermal."}, {"id": 36, "t": 10, "s": 0, "q": "A client is receiving streptomycin. Which finding indicates an adverse effect?", "o": ["Increased liver enzymes.", "Staining of the teeth.", "Creatinine of 2.2 mg/dL.", "Photosensitivity."], "a": 2, "r": "Aminoglycosides (“-mycin”) are nephrotoxic and ototoxic. Elevated creatinine shows kidney injury. Teeth staining/photosensitivity are tetracycline effects."}, {"id": 37, "t": 11, "s": 0, "q": "A client who had a spontaneous abortion is taught about Rho(D) immune globulin. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?", "o": ["“This medication will allow for safe mixing of my blood with the baby’s in future pregnancies.”", "“The medication could cause redness or mild pain at the injection site.”", "“This medication will prevent potential loss of pregnancy in the future.”", "“The medication will help my body fully remove the placenta lining.”"], "a": 3, "r": "RhoGAM prevents an Rh-negative mother from forming antibodies against Rh-positive fetal cells. It has no effect on removing the placenta or uterine lining. Injection-site soreness is expected."}, {"id": 38, "t": 7, "s": 0, "q": "A client with a history of PUD in the ED has BP 110/72, P 90, yellow emesis, frequent belching, Hgb 9.2 g/dL. What is the priority?", "o": ["Determine if the client has any stressors.", "Type and cross for a PRBC transfusion.", "Administer an IV antiemetic.", "Prepare the client for surgery."], "a": 2, "r": "Stable vital signs with no signs of active hemorrhage mean the immediate problem is vomiting. Controlling it prevents aspiration, dehydration and electrolyte loss. Compare: with hypotension (e.g., 92/58) bleeding is suspected and the priority changes."}, {"id": 39, "t": 11, "s": 0, "q": "During labor with fetal monitoring, the FHR has been 180 for 12 minutes. What should the nurse do first?", "o": ["Assist the client with guided imagery.", "Continue to monitor the FHR.", "Turn the client to their left side.", "Prepare the client for cesarean delivery."], "a": 2, "r": "FHR >160 for 10 minutes is fetal tachycardia (possible hypoxia). First, use the least invasive intervention: left lateral position to relieve cord compression and improve placental perfusion, then give oxygen, IV fluids, and notify the provider."}, {"id": 40, "t": 5, "s": 0, "q": "It is a priority to notify the PHCP if a client who", "o": ["has community-acquired pneumonia has WBC 15,000/mm³.", "takes warfarin has an INR of 2.", "has end-stage renal disease and a creatinine of 2.7 mg/dL.", "has heart failure and a sodium of 125 mEq/L."], "a": 3, "r": "Sodium 125 is critically low (normal 135–145) and risks seizures and confusion. Elevated WBC in pneumonia, INR 2 on warfarin, and elevated creatinine in ESRD are all expected."}, {"id": 41, "t": 5, "s": 0, "q": "A client 4 hours post-op after general anesthesia has T 98.4° F, P 58, BP 98/64, shallow respirations at 26, pale dry skin; ABG pH 7.20, PaCO₂ 54, HCO₃ 26. Which findings relate to the ABG? 1 IV fluids; 2 BP; 3 pulse; 4 temperature; 5 urine output; 6 general anesthesia; 7 pale dry skin; 8 shallow respirations.", "o": ["2, 3, 6, 7, 8", "2, 5, 6, 8", "1, 2, 4, 5, 6", "4, 5, 7, 8"], "a": 0, "r": "This is acute respiratory acidosis (low pH, high CO₂, normal HCO₃). Anesthesia depresses respiration → shallow breathing → CO₂ retention, with hypotension, bradycardia, and pale skin."}, {"id": 42, "t": 12, "s": 0, "q": "In a pediatric cardiac unit, which child should the nurse assess first?", "o": ["2-year-old walking in the playroom with a pulse of 150.", "6-year-old sitting and eating a snack with a pulse of 130.", "16-year-old sleeping with a pulse of 55.", "2-month-old crying with a pulse of 170."], "a": 1, "r": "Know pediatric norms. A resting 6-year-old should be ~75–120 bpm, so 130 is abnormal. The others are acceptable for activity/crying/sleeping."}, {"id": 43, "t": 15, "s": 0, "q": "A client with lung cancer is receiving external radiation therapy. Which statement shows understanding?", "o": ["“I will need to avoid eating raw fruits and vegetables.”", "“I should avoid close contact with anyone who is pregnant or children under 12.”", "“I will need to avoid direct skin exposure to the sun during treatment and for at least 1 year afterward.”", "“I should apply 50-SPF sunscreen to the radiation area before going out.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Irradiated skin stays sensitive for a year or more, so protect it from sun. External radiation does not make the client radioactive. Don’t apply products to the site unless prescribed."}, {"id": 44, "t": 5, "s": 0, "q": "A client had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has", "o": ["fluid intake of 1,000 mL and urine output of 800 mL over 8 hours.", "a temperature rise from 98.2 to 99.3° F over 4 hours.", "a WBC decrease from 6,000 to 5,000/mm³.", "a weight increase from 199 to 203 lb over 24 hours."], "a": 3, "r": "A gain of 4 lb in 24 hours (about 2 L of fluid) signals fluid retention and possible acute rejection. The other values are minor."}, {"id": 45, "t": 3, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client who develops the following rhythm (premature wide, bizarre QRS complexes with inverted T waves — PVCs). Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Determine if the client has an internal pacemaker.", "Check the client’s potassium level.", "Place a defibrillator at the bedside.", "Notify the rapid response team."], "a": 1, "r": "Isolated PVCs are commonly caused by electrolyte imbalance (especially low K⁺ or Mg²⁺), hypoxia, or ischemia. Check the potassium level and notify the provider if it is abnormal. (Rhythm strip was an image in your PDF.)"}, {"id": 46, "t": 7, "s": 0, "q": "A client had gastric surgery 8 hours ago and has an NG tube to low suction. Which interventions belong in the plan? 1 Turn down the suction when assessing for peristalsis. 2 Encourage IS every 4 hours while awake. 3 Maintain SCDs. 4 Irrigate the NG with warm tap water as needed. 5 Turn and reposition every 2 hours. 6 Place a sign: “Do not manipulate the NG tube.”", "o": ["1, 2, 3", "3, 5, 6", "2, 4, 5", "1, 3, 5"], "a": 1, "r": "After gastric surgery, the NG tube must not be moved or irrigated without a surgeon’s order (protect the suture line). SCDs and q2h turning prevent VTE and pressure injury. Suction should be turned OFF to auscultate. IS is needed every 1–2 hours, and irrigation uses sterile normal saline, never tap water."}, {"id": 47, "t": 4, "s": 0, "q": "After tracheostomy care, which finding requires intervention before leaving the room?", "o": ["The client places a finger over the tube opening when speaking.", "The cuff can be easily inflated and deflated.", "There is space for 1 finger between the tie and the neck.", "The tube moves in and out of the stoma when the client coughs."], "a": 3, "r": "Tube movement means the ties are too loose and the tube can dislodge or be expelled. Fix it. A one-finger gap is correct; occluding the opening to speak is expected (speaking valve/finger)."}, {"id": 48, "t": 4, "s": 0, "q": "A client on mechanical ventilation has a low-pressure alarm. Which action should the nurse take next?", "o": ["Shut the alarm off and call a code.", "Assess the cuff of the client’s endotracheal tube.", "Manually ventilate with a bag-mask-valve device.", "Call respiratory therapy to check the ventilator settings."], "a": 1, "r": "Low-pressure alarm = a leak: disconnected tubing, cuff leak, or flat pilot balloon. Assess the client and the cuff. Never silence the alarm without finding the cause."}, {"id": 49, "t": 12, "s": 0, "q": "The nurse provides discharge teaching to parents of a child admitted with sickle cell crisis. Which instruction should be included?", "o": ["“Monitor your child’s temperature daily.”", "“Restrict play activity to 1 hour per day.”", "“Avoid areas with low altitude.”", "“Maintain a warm temperature in your home.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Cold causes vasoconstriction and sickling, so keep the home warm and avoid cold exposure, dehydration, and HIGH altitude (low oxygen). Also report fever promptly. Don’t restrict normal activity."}, {"id": 50, "t": 13, "s": 0, "q": "An older adult with dementia often tells stories to cope with anxiety. The nurse recognizes confabulation when the client", "o": ["tells made-up stories.", "refuses to see family members.", "displays ritualistic behaviors.", "reminisces about the past."], "a": 0, "r": "Confabulation = unconsciously filling memory gaps with fabricated stories. Refusing family is withdrawal, rituals are compulsions, and reminiscing is normal life review."}, {"id": 51, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse on the CQI committee is developing a program to reduce medication errors after a sentinel event. Which strategy should the committee initiate first?", "o": ["Identify key performance indicators (KPIs) of the medication error.", "Review the events that led to the error by creating a flow diagram.", "Provide an inservice to staff on safe medication administration.", "Develop a quality improvement program for nurses involved in the error."], "a": 1, "r": "Understand the problem before acting. Mapping the process (flow diagram/root-cause analysis) shows where the system failed. Education and programs follow, and the focus is on systems, not blaming individuals."}, {"id": 52, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse attended a conference on informed consent. Which statement indicates correct understanding?", "o": ["Informed consent can be waived per facility policy for urgent treatment if the client is unconscious.", "Informed consent may not be withdrawn once the client signs.", "Informed consent can be signed by a client with dementia if oriented at the time of signing.", "Informed consent is signed by the nurse to indicate the client understands."], "a": 0, "r": "Emergency (implied) consent applies when delay would risk life or limb and the client cannot consent. Consent can be withdrawn at any time, the client must be competent, and the nurse witnesses the signature but the provider explains the procedure."}, {"id": 53, "t": 0, "s": 1, "q": "After hand-off report, the nurse should first see the client who", "o": ["has pancreatitis and reports mid-epigastric discomfort.", "had a transesophageal echocardiogram 2 hours ago, BP 106/64, with a sore throat.", "has cirrhosis, new ecchymosis, and PTT 76 seconds.", "had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy 18 hours ago with bilateral shoulder pain."], "a": 2, "r": "A PTT of 76 s (normal 25–35) with new bruising means active bleeding risk and a coagulation problem in cirrhosis. Epigastric discomfort, post-TEE sore throat, and shoulder pain from retained CO₂ after laparoscopy are expected."}, {"id": 54, "t": 0, "s": 1, "q": "Clients are 24 hours postoperative; the nurse assessed 4 hours ago. Which change is a priority?", "o": ["Received NPH insulin and capillary glucose is 110 mg/dL.", "Wound drainage was sanguineous and is now serosanguineous.", "BP was 138/86 and is now 136/84.", "Reports pain increased to 8/10 after an opioid analgesic."], "a": 3, "r": "Pain that worsens after an opioid can signal a complication (bleeding, ischemia, compartment syndrome, anastomotic leak). The other changes are normal or insignificant."}, {"id": 55, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "The preceptor is teaching legal principles. Which situation is an example of malpractice?", "o": ["An alert client leaves AMA; the nurse applies restraints to prevent leaving.", "A competent client refuses an antidepressant; the nurse dissolves it in food and gives it without the client’s knowledge.", "The nurse plugs in an alarming IV pump despite noticing a loose outlet cover.", "A client on a low-sodium diet eats chips; the nurse threatens wrist restraints."], "a": 1, "r": "Giving medication covertly violates the client’s right to refuse treatment and falls below the standard of care. Items A and D are false imprisonment/assault concerns. (Key per source.)"}, {"id": 56, "t": 0, "s": 1, "q": "It is a priority for the nurse to follow up with a client who", "o": ["is receiving hydrocortisone and has glucose 135 mg/dL.", "has MS, feels tired, and has diplopia.", "has active pulmonary TB, hemoptysis, and weight loss.", "is receiving pyridostigmine and reports nausea and a congested cough."], "a": 3, "r": "Nausea and increased secretions on an anticholinesterase suggest cholinergic crisis (overmedication). This can progress to respiratory failure. The other findings are expected in those diagnoses."}, {"id": 57, "t": 12, "s": 1, "q": "On a pediatric cardiac unit, which client should the nurse initially assess?", "o": ["5-year-old watching TV with a pulse of 78.", "Toddler playing with toys with a pulse of 100.", "1-month-old sleeping with a pulse of 180.", "15-year-old reading with a pulse of 60."], "a": 2, "r": "An infant’s normal rate is ~100–160, lower when asleep. 180 while sleeping is tachycardia. The other rates are normal."}, {"id": 58, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "Which child should receive an interdisciplinary conference?", "o": ["3 years old, epiglottitis, difficulty breathing.", "4 years old, new Kawasaki disease, started on immunoglobulin.", "8 years old, multiple compound fractures in Buck’s traction.", "5 years old, cystic fibrosis since birth, hospitalized 4 times in 2 months."], "a": 3, "r": "A chronic, complex illness with repeated admissions needs a team approach (respiratory therapy, dietitian, PT, social work, spiritual care). Others are acute or have a single-discipline focus."}, {"id": 59, "t": 3, "s": 1, "q": "A client with pericarditis has been hospitalized for 24 hours. WBC 13,000/mm³ and temperature 100.8° F. What is the most appropriate action?", "o": ["Update the nursing interventions as needed.", "Notify the charge nurse that the plan of care is outdated.", "Change the client’s goal to reflect current status.", "Obtain an order for an infectious disease referral."], "a": 0, "r": "These findings fit the inflammatory process of pericarditis. Evaluation of the plan means modifying interventions (e.g., fever/comfort measures), not changing goals or escalating."}, {"id": 60, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "Which charted note by a nurse indicates correct documentation?", "o": ["“Dressing change performed to coccyx wound with no adverse effects noted by the client. Client transferred to chair.”", "“IV catheter site in left antecubital space is red and warm to touch. IV catheter removed intact. 20-gauge catheter placed in right forearm.”", "“Found client sitting on floor. All 4 side rails upright. Client reports no pain. No abrasions or bleeding. PHCP notified. Incident report completed.”", "“Reports of pain in abdomen with vomiting noted. Medications administered.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Document objective, specific, factual details (site, assessment findings, gauge, location). Don’t mention incident reports in the chart, and avoid vague wording (“no adverse effects,” “medications administered”)."}, {"id": 61, "t": 1, "s": 1, "q": "A client brought in their own CPAP machine. What is the most important nurse action?", "o": ["Explain that the hospital must supply the CPAP machine.", "Review the instruction booklet on how to operate the machine.", "Ask the client when they last cleaned the machine.", "Have the machine inspected by the facility’s engineering department."], "a": 3, "r": "Client-owned electrical equipment must be checked for electrical safety by biomedical/engineering before use."}, {"id": 62, "t": 2, "s": 1, "q": "The facility’s emergency response plan is activated. Which client should the nurse recommend for discharge?", "o": ["DVT on warfarin with PT 28 seconds.", "HIV positive, nonproductive cough, Mantoux 6 mm induration.", "Suspected appendicitis with intense McBurney’s point pain, now reporting the pain has stopped.", "COPD on IV steroids with SpO₂ 86% on room air."], "a": 0, "r": "Discharge the most stable client. A PT of 28 s (about 2× normal) is therapeutic on warfarin. Sudden pain relief in appendicitis suggests rupture, 6 mm in an HIV+ client is positive, and SpO₂ 86% is unstable."}, {"id": 63, "t": 1, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse observes a newly hired UAP. Which action indicates correct care?", "o": ["Wearing a gown to enter the room of a client with influenza.", "Removing protective eyewear before removing gloves.", "Placing dedicated equipment in the room of a client with herpes zoster.", "Donning sterile gloves to empty a urinary drainage device."], "a": 2, "r": "Herpes zoster requires contact precautions, including dedicated equipment. Influenza = droplet (mask). Gloves come off FIRST (most contaminated), and clean gloves are used to empty drainage devices."}, {"id": 64, "t": 1, "s": 1, "q": "During a class on child car passenger safety, which parent statement indicates a need for further teaching?", "o": ["“Car seats should be rear-facing in the back seat until about 2 to 4 years.”", "“With a booster seat, the lap belt should be across the child’s upper thighs.”", "“The harness chest clip should be at the level of the infant’s armpits.”", "“One hand should fit between the infant and the harness.”"], "a": 3, "r": "The harness should be snug: no more than one to two fingers should fit beneath it. A whole hand means it is too loose."}, {"id": 65, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "Which new order requires follow-up by the nurse?", "o": ["Fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour for a 12-year-old in sickle cell crisis.", "High-potassium diet for a 5-year-old with acute glomerulonephritis.", "Chelation therapy for an 8-year-old with lead poisoning.", "IV hydrocortisone for a 10-year-old with adrenal insufficiency."], "a": 1, "r": "Acute glomerulonephritis causes oliguria and hyperkalemia, so potassium, sodium, and fluids are restricted. The other orders are appropriate."}, {"id": 66, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "Which order should the nurse clarify?", "o": ["IV alteplase for ischemic stroke symptoms that began 2 hours ago.", "Low-sodium, low-potassium diet for acute kidney injury.", "Chest x-ray for a client with a permanent pacemaker placed 6 months ago.", "1 unit of PRBCs for a client with polycythemia vera."], "a": 3, "r": "Polycythemia vera is an excess of RBCs, so transfusing more would increase viscosity and clotting risk. X-rays are safe with pacemakers; MRI is the concern."}, {"id": 67, "t": 11, "s": 1, "q": "Oxytocin is infusing for labor augmentation. The nurse should discontinue it if the client has", "o": ["uterine relaxation between contractions.", "an FHR of 68 for 90 seconds.", "dark red streaks in the bloody show.", "intermittent back pain with each contraction."], "a": 1, "r": "Fetal bradycardia indicates fetal distress or uterine hyperstimulation. Stop oxytocin, turn the client left, give oxygen, and notify the provider. Relaxation between contractions is desired."}, {"id": 68, "t": 11, "s": 1, "q": "After ensuring a newborn has a patent airway, what should the nurse do next?", "o": ["Weigh the newborn and document the weight.", "Dry the newborn’s skin and cover with a warm blanket.", "Administer vitamin K.", "Administer eye prophylaxis."], "a": 1, "r": "After airway comes thermoregulation (ABC then temperature). Cold stress increases oxygen consumption and hypoglycemia. Weight, vitamin K and eye care follow."}, {"id": 69, "t": 16, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse is auscultating the aortic valve. The diagram shows four sites: (1) right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border, (2) left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border, (3) left 4th intercostal space at the sternal border, (4) left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line. Where should the stethoscope be placed?", "o": ["Site 1: right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border.", "Site 2: left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border.", "Site 3: left 4th intercostal space, sternal border.", "Site 4: left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line."], "a": 0, "r": "Aortic = right 2nd ICS (“Aortic Right”). Pulmonic = left 2nd ICS. Tricuspid = left lower sternal border. Mitral (apical) = 5th ICS midclavicular line. (Diagram was an image in your PDF.)"}, {"id": 70, "t": 11, "s": 1, "q": "A client with AB-negative blood delivered a baby 4 hours ago. Which action should the nurse be prepared to take?", "o": ["Assess the newborn for abnormal clotting.", "Administer Rh(D) immune globulin to the infant after double-nurse verification.", "Review the newborn’s blood type.", "Administer Rh(D) immune globulin to the mother within 24 hours of birth."], "a": 2, "r": "Rh(D) immune globulin is given to the Rh-NEGATIVE mother only if the newborn is Rh-POSITIVE, so check the newborn’s type first. It is given to the mother within 72 hours (not the infant)."}, {"id": 71, "t": 11, "s": 1, "q": "LMP began November 19. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the EDD?", "o": ["July 12", "August 12", "July 26", "August 26"], "a": 3, "r": "Naegele’s rule: add 7 days to the LMP and subtract 3 months. Nov 19 + 7 = Nov 26 → minus 3 months = August 26."}, {"id": 72, "t": 13, "s": 1, "q": "A client who had a below-the-knee amputation from diabetic circulation problems shows ineffective coping with defense mechanisms. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Determine what is causing the client to respond in such a way.", "Obtain an order for a psychiatric consultation.", "Tell the client their behavior is not acceptable.", "Explain that their reactions are unproductive to getting better."], "a": 0, "r": "Assess first (nursing process) with open, nonjudgmental exploration. Defense mechanisms are normal reactions to loss. Avoid judging or lecturing."}, {"id": 73, "t": 13, "s": 1, "q": "A client presents to the ED after an amitriptyline overdose. Which action should the nurse take first?", "o": ["Administer oxygen via nasal cannula.", "Initiate gastric lavage with activated charcoal.", "Determine the events leading to the overdose.", "Document the number of pills ingested."], "a": 0, "r": "ABCs come first: TCA overdose causes respiratory depression, arrhythmias, and seizures. Support airway/breathing before decontamination or history-taking."}, {"id": 74, "t": 13, "s": 1, "q": "On a mental health unit, the nurse should first assess the client who has", "o": ["paranoid schizophrenia and has not bathed in 3 days.", "major depressive disorder stating, “I wish I could stop crying all the time.”", "bulimia nervosa and went to the restroom 15 minutes after a meal.", "suicidal ideation and is reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with staff."], "a": 2, "r": "Active purging (vomiting) risks electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia) and cardiac arrhythmias. The suicidal client is already engaged with staff in safety planning, and the others are not urgent."}, {"id": 75, "t": 13, "s": 1, "q": "Which client is at highest risk for suicide?", "o": ["70-year-old man whose wife recently died and goes to weekly bingo with friends.", "52-year-old man who lost his job 3 months ago and feels he will never find another.", "39-year-old woman newly diagnosed with breast cancer seeking a second opinion.", "15-year-old girl who just broke up with her boyfriend and spends more time with friends."], "a": 1, "r": "Hopelessness plus a major loss in a middle-aged man is a strong risk profile. The others still have social connection and active coping."}, {"id": 76, "t": 7, "s": 1, "q": "A newly hired nurse is giving continuous tube feeding via a gastrostomy tube. Which action requires immediate intervention?", "o": ["Adding no more than 4 hours’ worth of feeding to the bag.", "Keeping the HOB at 30–45 degrees.", "Using an irrigation set opened 48 hours ago.", "Returning the residual obtained from the tube."], "a": 2, "r": "Irrigation sets are changed every 24 hours to prevent bacterial contamination. Limiting formula to 4 hours, a 30–45° HOB, and returning residuals are acceptable."}, {"id": 77, "t": 5, "s": 1, "q": "A client with urinary incontinence is at risk for skin breakdown. Which preventive action should the nurse implement?", "o": ["Check the skin every 8 hours.", "Request an order for an indwelling catheter.", "Clean the perineum with hot water after each episode.", "Apply a moisture barrier ointment to the skin."], "a": 3, "r": "Barrier ointment protects skin from urine. Check and cleanse after each episode with warm water and pH-balanced cleanser. Catheters are not used for convenience (CAUTI risk)."}, {"id": 78, "t": 8, "s": 1, "q": "Which action should the nurse take when caring for a client in Buck’s (skin) traction?", "o": ["Turn the client to a 30-degree angle every 2 hours.", "Provide pin site care.", "Assess pedal push strength every 4 hours.", "Keep the foot of the bed elevated."], "a": 3, "r": "Buck’s traction needs countertraction: elevate the foot of the bed so the client’s body weight pulls back against the traction. Pin care applies to skeletal traction."}, {"id": 79, "t": 5, "s": 1, "q": "When assessing an older adult for urinary incontinence, which question is important?", "o": ["“Do you take the herbal preparation ginkgo biloba?”", "“Do you ever use pads or tissues in your underwear to catch urine?”", "“Do you have a history of kidney stones?”", "“Do you have a family history of polycystic kidney disease?”"], "a": 1, "r": "Asking about pads/tissues reveals the extent, frequency, and impact of incontinence, which is essential for planning care."}, {"id": 80, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "A client with a UTI has been prescribed ciprofloxacin. Which instruction should the nurse give?", "o": ["“This medication provides an analgesic effect and decreases painful urination.”", "“Antacids should be taken 30 minutes after this medication.”", "“You should not operate heavy machinery or drive while taking this medication.”", "“You could experience dizziness and should change positions slowly.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Fluoroquinolones can cause dizziness, lightheadedness, and CNS effects, so teach safety and slow position changes. Also report tendon pain (rupture risk), and separate from antacids/iron/calcium by 2+ hours."}, {"id": 81, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "An older adult takes furosemide daily for heart failure. Which finding is a priority to follow up?", "o": ["Reports of ringing in the ears.", "Orientation to person and place only.", "Potassium level of 3.5 mEq/L.", "Weight loss of 1 lb in 3 days."], "a": 0, "r": "Tinnitus signals ototoxicity, which can become permanent with loop diuretics, so report it before further doses. Potassium 3.5 is low-normal and a 1 lb loss is expected. (Key per source.)"}, {"id": 82, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "A 22-year-old female is prescribed lithium. Which statement needs further teaching?", "o": ["“I should be sure to eat food that contains sodium.”", "“I should take this medication with food.”", "“This medication will make my urine turn reddish-orange.”", "“I should avoid becoming dehydrated.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Lithium does not discolor urine (that’s phenazopyridine/rifampin). Maintain steady sodium and fluid intake, because low sodium or dehydration raises lithium to toxic levels."}, {"id": 83, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "A client’s potassium is 2.4 mEq/L. Which order should the nurse question?", "o": ["Administer 20 mEq KCl IV over 15 minutes.", "Withhold the regularly scheduled furosemide.", "Place the client on telemetry.", "Allow the client out of bed with assistance only."], "a": 0, "r": "IV potassium must not exceed 10 mEq/hr on a general unit (rapid infusion can cause cardiac arrest). Telemetry, holding furosemide, and fall precautions are appropriate."}, {"id": 84, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "Which prescription should the nurse follow up with the PHCP?", "o": ["Aspirin sensitivity and prescribed acetaminophen.", "Potassium 3.8 mEq/L and prescribed sodium polystyrene.", "Decreased phenytoin level and prescribed seizure precautions.", "History of rash with penicillin and prescribed an MAOI."], "a": 1, "r": "Sodium polystyrene lowers potassium and is for hyperkalemia. A normal K⁺ of 3.8 could cause hypokalemia. The other orders are appropriate (the penicillin rash is unrelated to MAOIs)."}, {"id": 85, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse is teaching a client with anemia who is starting liquid ferrous sulfate. Which instruction should be included?", "o": ["“Avoid eating excessive amounts of beans and green leafy vegetables.”", "“You will notice a change in your bowel habits and stool color.”", "“The medication can be taken with milk to prevent GI irritation.”", "“Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Iron causes dark green/black stools and constipation or GI upset. Dilute liquid iron, use a straw to avoid staining teeth, and avoid milk/antacids that reduce absorption."}, {"id": 86, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse will give subcutaneous enoxaparin. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Check the most current D-dimer.", "Gently massage the area after administration.", "Aspirate for blood before injecting.", "Insert the needle at a 90-degree angle."], "a": 3, "r": "Enoxaparin is given in the abdomen at 90°. Don’t aspirate, expel the air bubble, or massage (bruising/hematoma). Monitor platelets/CBC, not D-dimer."}, {"id": 87, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "A toddler who weighs 22 lb is prescribed ampicillin 150 mg PO three times daily. Available: suspension 250 mg/5 mL. What should the nurse do?", "o": ["Check for an allergy to sulfa medications.", "Contact the PHCP for clarification.", "Administer 3 mL by mouth 3 times per day.", "Determine the toddler’s weight in kilograms."], "a": 2, "r": "Do the math: 150 mg ÷ 250 mg × 5 mL = 3 mL per dose. The question supplies what is needed, so no clarification is required (22 lb ≈ 10 kg)."}, {"id": 88, "t": 7, "s": 1, "q": "After a paracentesis, it is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client develops", "o": ["pink-tinged urine.", "vesicular breath sounds.", "flatulence.", "pruritus."], "a": 0, "r": "Pink-tinged urine (hematuria) suggests bladder puncture/perforation. Vesicular breath sounds are normal, and expected findings include drainage at the site and decreased abdominal girth."}, {"id": 89, "t": 5, "s": 1, "q": "ABG: pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 26 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 24 mEq/L. Which acid-base imbalance?", "o": ["Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"], "a": 2, "r": "High pH (alkalosis) with LOW CO₂ (respiratory cause) and normal bicarbonate = respiratory alkalosis, from hyperventilation (anxiety, pain, hypoxia)."}, {"id": 90, "t": 13, "s": 1, "q": "A client admitted for alcohol withdrawal now (1500) has T 102° F, P 120, BP 164/100, profuse diaphoresis, hallucinations, nausea and vomiting (earlier vitals were near-normal with mild agitation). What action is appropriate?", "o": ["Administer flumazenil.", "Notify the PHCP.", "Attempt to orient the client.", "Increase the rate of the IV infusion."], "a": 1, "r": "This pattern indicates delirium tremens, a life-threatening emergency. The client needs more benzodiazepines, so notify the provider immediately. Flumazenil (a benzodiazepine antagonist) can trigger seizures."}, {"id": 91, "t": 7, "s": 1, "q": "During a large-volume enema, the client’s abdomen becomes distended and rigid. What should the nurse do?", "o": ["Reposition the client for better instillation.", "Lower the enema fluid bag.", "Ask the client to rate their pain level.", "Discontinue the enema treatment."], "a": 3, "r": "A distended, rigid abdomen suggests bowel perforation. Stop immediately and notify the provider."}, {"id": 92, "t": 16, "s": 1, "q": "A female client was taught to collect a midstream urine specimen. Which statement requires additional teaching?", "o": ["“I will remove the container before stopping the flow of urine.”", "“Since I’m right-handed, I will use my left hand to spread the labia.”", "“I will use the towelette to cleanse the area using a back-and-forth motion.”", "“I will start to urinate in the toilet before urinating into the container.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Cleanse from front to back, using a new wipe each time. Back-and-forth spreads organisms toward the urethra."}, {"id": 93, "t": 6, "s": 1, "q": "A client with Addison disease is at risk for Addisonian crisis. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Restrict fluid intake.", "Administer hydrocortisone.", "Provide a low-carbohydrate diet.", "Monitor the client’s ADH levels."], "a": 1, "r": "Addison disease = cortisol/aldosterone deficiency; crisis causes hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia. Treatment is IV hydrocortisone and fluids, not restriction."}, {"id": 94, "t": 12, "s": 1, "q": "Which finding should the nurse instruct parents to report after their child’s tonsillectomy?", "o": ["Odor to the breath.", "Persistent earache.", "Mouth breathing.", "Temperature of 99.4° F."], "a": 1, "r": "Bad breath and mouth breathing are expected after tonsillectomy, and a low-grade temp is normal. Persistent earache may signal infection and needs to be reported (also report frequent swallowing and bright red bleeding)."}, {"id": 95, "t": 3, "s": 1, "q": "A telemetry client continues to develop the rhythm shown (irregularly irregular, narrow QRS, no distinct P waves — atrial fibrillation). What should the nurse plan?", "o": ["Prepare to administer digoxin.", "Initiate transcutaneous pacing.", "Defibrillate the client.", "Administer IV atropine."], "a": 0, "r": "Atrial fibrillation with a stable client is treated with rate control (digoxin, β-blockers, calcium channel blockers) and anticoagulation. Pacing/atropine are for bradycardia, and defibrillation is for VF/pulseless VT. (Strip was an image.)"}, {"id": 96, "t": 5, "s": 1, "q": "Which signs and symptoms of peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis should the nurse discuss? 1 Hypoactive bowel sounds 2 Rebound abdominal tenderness 3 Amber urine 4 Nausea and vomiting 5 Cloudy or opaque outflow 6 Diarrhea 7 Bradycardia", "o": ["2, 5, 6, 7", "1, 4, 7", "4", "1, 2, 4, 5"], "a": 3, "r": "Peritonitis: cloudy effluent, abdominal pain/rebound tenderness, fever, N/V, hypoactive bowel sounds (ileus), and tachycardia. Not bradycardia."}, {"id": 97, "t": 8, "s": 1, "q": "A client suspected of having a retinal detachment of the LEFT eye. Which statement is consistent?", "o": ["“I feel as if a curtain has been pulled down over my right eye.”", "“I have lost the central part of my vision.”", "“I am experiencing a sharp pain in my eye.”", "“I see floating dark spots in my left eye.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Retinal detachment is painless with floaters, flashes, and a curtain/veil in the affected eye. Central vision loss suggests macular degeneration."}, {"id": 98, "t": 16, "s": 1, "q": "The nurse has an order to remove intermittent sutures from an abdominal wound. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Remove each suture in order from top to bottom.", "Cut the suture by lifting it with forceps and cutting as close to the skin as possible.", "Send the removed sutures to the lab for culture and sensitivity.", "Apply adhesive strips after each suture is removed."], "a": 1, "r": "Lift the knot, cut close to the skin on the side opposite the knot, and pull gently so no exposed (contaminated) suture is dragged through tissue. Remove alternating sutures first, then apply strips as needed."}, {"id": 99, "t": 10, "s": 1, "q": "A client with a pulmonary embolism on heparin. Which lab value requires immediate attention?", "o": ["PT 12 seconds", "aPTT 60 seconds", "Hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL", "Platelets 100,000/mm³"], "a": 3, "r": "A falling platelet count (<100,000 or >50% drop) suggests heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Stop heparin and notify the provider. aPTT 60 s is therapeutic (1.5–2.5× control)."}, {"id": 100, "t": 9, "s": 1, "q": "A client has deep partial-thickness burns to the lower torso, both forearms, and thighs from a hot grease spill. Which action is a priority?", "o": ["Maintaining a warm environment.", "Inserting an indwelling urinary catheter.", "Preparing to intubate the client.", "Inspecting for signs of inhalation injury."], "a": 1, "r": "This is a large burn (~30%+ TBSA) needing fluid resuscitation, and hourly urine output (≥30 mL/hr) is the best indicator that resuscitation is adequate. A scald from hot grease has no inhalation mechanism. (Key per source.)"}, {"id": 101, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "A client scheduled for ECT tomorrow asks, “Can I refuse the ECT treatment tomorrow?” Which response should the nurse make?", "o": ["“You should consider discussing this decision with your family first.”", "“Having this treatment can be scary, but it is going to help you feel better.”", "“Let’s talk with your doctor before you make a final decision.”", "“You have a right to refuse even though you have signed the consent.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Clients can withdraw consent at any time. The nurse is a client advocate and must not coerce, reassure falsely, or delay the client’s decision."}, {"id": 102, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "Which statement indicates correct understanding of informed consent?", "o": ["It is an agreement to receive medical treatment that cannot be revoked once signed.", "Forms must be signed when a client is competent to make an informed decision.", "It is signed by the nurse to indicate the client understands the procedure.", "It is the nurse’s responsibility to explain the risks, benefits, and alternatives."], "a": 1, "r": "The client must be competent (capacity) to consent, and consent can be revoked. The provider explains risks/benefits/alternatives, and the nurse only witnesses the signature."}, {"id": 103, "t": 0, "s": 2, "q": "After change-of-shift report, the nurse should first see the client who", "o": ["had laparoscopic surgery 12 hours ago with right shoulder pain.", "had a cardiac catheterization 1 hour ago and has been drowsy since the procedure.", "is scheduled for an echocardiogram in 1 hour and is eating food from a visitor.", "had an ERCP 1 hour ago and is reporting difficulty swallowing."], "a": 3, "r": "Difficulty swallowing after ERCP (sedation, throat anesthesia, or duodenal perforation) threatens the airway — ABCs. Referred shoulder pain after laparoscopy is expected, and a drowsy client after sedation is not unusual."}, {"id": 104, "t": 11, "s": 2, "q": "In L&D, several clients are in second-stage labor on external monitors. Which client is a priority for follow-up?", "o": ["Cervix dilated to 10 cm with moderately intense contractions of 40–50 seconds.", "FHR consistent at 132 bpm.", "Bloody show that is dark red with active pushing.", "Contractions lasting 60 seconds with a 3-minute interval."], "a": 2, "r": "Bloody show should be pink or red-tinged mucus. Dark red bleeding could indicate abruptio placentae or uterine problems. The other findings are normal for second stage."}, {"id": 105, "t": 0, "s": 2, "q": "The RN is assigning tasks. Which is best to assign to the LPN/VN?", "o": ["Collection of past medical history for a client admitted for cellulitis.", "Recording output for a client who urinated in the toilet collection container.", "Assisting with delivery of breakfast trays.", "Developing a plan of care for a client postoperative from a cholecystectomy."], "a": 0, "r": "LPN/VNs may collect data and contribute to the plan of care. UAPs record output and deliver trays. The RN is responsible for developing the care plan."}, {"id": 106, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "Which client information requires the ED nurse to report to an outside agency?", "o": ["18-year-old at 4 months pregnant with abdominal pain.", "27-year-old with a history of gonorrhea and chlamydia from 1 year ago.", "45-year-old injured in a factory fire with 50% TBSA burns.", "72-year-old living with their daughter who has several stage 3 pressure ulcers."], "a": 3, "r": "Several stage 3 pressure ulcers in a dependent older adult suggest neglect/abuse, which is mandated reporting (Adult Protective Services). Past STIs aren’t reportable now."}, {"id": 107, "t": 0, "s": 2, "q": "On the pediatric unit, which child should the nurse assess first?", "o": ["15-year-old with acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and crackles with neck vein distention.", "13-year-old with hemophilia requiring prolonged pressure after a venipuncture.", "10-year-old with sickle cell disease reporting pain of 7/10 and due for pain medication.", "7-year-old with celiac disease and 3 episodes of diarrhea in 8 hours."], "a": 0, "r": "Crackles and JVD signal fluid overload/heart failure and pulmonary edema in PSGN. This threatens breathing and needs immediate assessment. Others are expected or being managed."}, {"id": 108, "t": 5, "s": 2, "q": "A client had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has", "o": ["urine that continues to be slightly pink since surgery.", "an increase in weight from 199 to 201 lb over 72 hours.", "a BUN change from 10 to 16 mg/dL over 48 hours.", "a BP change from 122/78 to 92/58 mm Hg over 4 hours."], "a": 3, "r": "A significant BP drop suggests hypovolemia, hemorrhage, or sepsis, which threatens graft perfusion. Pink urine is expected early after surgery, and a 2 lb gain over 72 hours is minor."}, {"id": 109, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "After assessing a newly admitted client with pericarditis, which action should the nurse take first when preparing the plan of care?", "o": ["Prioritize the client’s reported concerns.", "Determine any referrals that need to be initiated.", "Formulate short-term and long-term goals.", "Review client data to identify areas of concern."], "a": 3, "r": "Nursing process: analyze the data first, identify the problems/nursing diagnoses, and then set priorities, goals, and interventions."}, {"id": 110, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "Which documentation by a staff nurse indicates correct charting?", "o": ["“IV site in antecubital space is red and warm. IV removed. New IV started.”", "“Found client sitting on floor. Rails up. No pain. PHCP notified. Incident report completed.”", "“Returned from radiology. VS obtained with slight increase in BP.”", "“Ambulated from bed to doorway without assistance. No shortness of breath or diaphoresis noted. VS remain within baseline after ambulating.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Good documentation is specific, objective, and measurable (activity, response, vitals). Avoid vague terms like “slight increase,” omitted details, and references to incident reports."}, {"id": 111, "t": 2, "s": 2, "q": "Which situation requires the nurse to complete an incident report?", "o": ["A client developed phlebitis from an IV catheter and is now receiving antibiotics.", "A staff member called a code for a client in respiratory distress.", "A client reports having an advance directive but has no copy on the chart.", "The nurse finds a PRN prescription for restraints in the medical record."], "a": 0, "r": "Incident reports document unexpected events/adverse outcomes (e.g., IV complications requiring treatment). Calling a code is appropriate care, and the others are routine."}, {"id": 112, "t": 1, "s": 2, "q": "A newly hired UAP is observed. Which action indicates correct client care?", "o": ["Donning sterile gloves to empty a urinary drainage device.", "Removing protective eyewear before removing gloves.", "Wearing a gown, mask, and gloves when entering the room of a client with influenza.", "Placing dedicated equipment in the room of a client with cellulitis of the left leg."], "a": 2, "r": "Influenza requires droplet precautions (mask) plus standard precautions (gloves, gown if splash anticipated), so this is the only acceptable choice. Gloves come off first, and drainage emptying uses clean gloves."}, {"id": 113, "t": 1, "s": 2, "q": "In an ambulatory center, which client should be removed from the waiting room immediately?", "o": ["Reports burning and pruritus with a linear rash of vesicles around the right rib cage.", "A 1 mm flat, red area at a Mantoux site given 48 hours ago.", "Congestive heart failure and a 3 lb gain in 3 days.", "Fever of 99.6° F, generalized pain, and borderline personality disorder."], "a": 0, "r": "Herpes zoster (shingles) lesions are infectious to anyone without varicella immunity; place the client in an exam room under contact precautions."}, {"id": 114, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "Which prescription should the nurse follow up on?", "o": ["Filgrastim for a client with WBC 2,500/mm³.", "Preoperative atropine for a client with glaucoma.", "Fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour for a child in sickle cell crisis.", "Low-potassium diet for a child with acute glomerulonephritis."], "a": 1, "r": "Anticholinergics like atropine dilate the pupil and raise intraocular pressure (contraindicated in narrow-angle glaucoma). Filgrastim treats neutropenia, hydration helps sickle cell crisis, and K⁺ is restricted in AGN."}, {"id": 115, "t": 0, "s": 2, "q": "A client postoperative from a colon resection arrives from the PACU, responds to commands, and reports moderate pain. Which action should the nurse take first?", "o": ["Confirm the client’s identity.", "Assess the client’s surgical bandage.", "Auscultate the client’s abdomen.", "Evaluate the client’s vital signs."], "a": 0, "r": "On arrival, verify identity with two identifiers before any assessment or care (patient-safety first); then assess ABCs/vitals, and so on. (Marked with a “?” on your copy.)"}, {"id": 116, "t": 11, "s": 2, "q": "The nurse is caring for a newborn with hyperbilirubinemia receiving phototherapy. Which intervention should be included?", "o": ["Cover the newborn with a light blanket.", "Maintain the newborn on their back.", "Remove the eye shields each shift or during feedings.", "Provide a quiet, stimulus-free environment."], "a": 2, "r": "Eye shields protect the retinas but are removed during feeding/each shift to assess the eyes and promote bonding. Expose maximum skin (no blankets) and reposition regularly."}, {"id": 117, "t": 12, "s": 2, "q": "Which finding indicates a developmental delay?", "o": ["1-month-old who does not have head control.", "3-month-old who is unable to grasp objects.", "9-month-old who is unsteady when standing.", "14-month-old who is unable to drink from a cup."], "a": 3, "r": "By about 12–15 months toddlers can drink from a cup. Lack of head control is expected at 1 month, voluntary grasp appears ~4–5 months, and standing unsteadily at 9 months is normal."}, {"id": 118, "t": 11, "s": 2, "q": "LMP began August 26. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the EDD?", "o": ["May 19", "May 26", "June 2", "June 9"], "a": 2, "r": "Add 7 days → September 2; subtract 3 months → June 2."}, {"id": 119, "t": 15, "s": 2, "q": "A client with breast cancer had a mastectomy and is receiving chemotherapy. Which statement indicates effective coping?", "o": ["“I am glad the nausea and vomiting are subsiding.”", "“I do not need as much pain medication as prescribed.”", "“I made an appointment to get fitted for a prosthesis.”", "“I will begin the next round of chemotherapy next week.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Taking active steps toward adapting to body-image change shows effective coping. The other statements are only observations of symptoms/treatment."}, {"id": 120, "t": 13, "s": 2, "q": "A client with paranoid schizophrenia has lost 15 lb in 3 weeks and accuses staff of trying to poison them. Which intervention is a priority?", "o": ["Ask the client what foods they would like to eat.", "Allow the client to observe how the food is prepared.", "Provide the client with prepackaged foods that they like.", "Offer to sit with the client during meals."], "a": 2, "r": "Individually packaged foods the client can open themselves reduce suspicion of tampering, so the client can eat safely and gain weight."}, {"id": 121, "t": 13, "s": 2, "q": "A newly admitted client has major depressive disorder after the death of her newborn 2 weeks ago. Which goal is the priority?", "o": ["Signing a no-harm contract.", "Exhibiting expected grieving behaviors.", "Identifying positive qualities about herself.", "Assuming an active role in the care planning process."], "a": 0, "r": "Safety is the priority in depression with a major loss. Address suicide risk first; grieving, self-esteem, and participation come after."}, {"id": 122, "t": 7, "s": 2, "q": "A home health nurse is talking to the spouse of a client receiving intermittent PEG tube feedings. Which statement indicates correct understanding?", "o": ["“I check placement by checking gastric pH before each feeding.”", "“I make sure my spouse sits up for at least an hour after the feeding.”", "“I clean the reusable feeding system with soap and hot water every 48 hours.”", "“I withhold the feeding if the gastric residual is 50 mL.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Remain upright for 30–60 minutes after feeding to prevent aspiration. Home PEG placement isn’t checked with pH, systems are washed after each use, and residual thresholds are higher (typically hold at ≥250 mL in adults)."}, {"id": 123, "t": 3, "s": 2, "q": "A client on bed rest. Which action should the nurse take to help prevent venous thromboembolism?", "o": ["Place the client on an alternating pressure relieving mattress.", "Maintain the HOB at 60 degrees or higher.", "Instruct the client to cough and deep breathe every hour.", "Ensure the client maintains adequate hydration."], "a": 3, "r": "Hydration prevents hemoconcentration and venous stasis. Mattresses prevent pressure injuries, and coughing/deep breathing prevents atelectasis. (Also use SCDs, leg exercises and early ambulation.)"}, {"id": 124, "t": 8, "s": 2, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client in skeletal traction. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Maintain the bed in a reverse-Trendelenburg position.", "Provide pin site care as prescribed.", "Assess pedal push strength every 4 hours.", "Turn the client to a 30-degree angle every 2 hours."], "a": 1, "r": "Skeletal traction uses pins through bone, with a high risk of osteomyelitis, so pin care is essential. The pulleys, ropes, and weights must hang freely."}, {"id": 125, "t": 5, "s": 2, "q": "A client is 12 hours post TURP. Which should be in the plan of care?", "o": ["Monitor hemoglobin and hematocrit levels.", "Restrict oral fluids while receiving continuous bladder irrigation.", "Notify the PHCP if the client has blood-tinged urine.", "Perform intermittent irrigation of the catheter with sterile saline every hour."], "a": 0, "r": "Hemorrhage is the main complication after TURP. Pink-tinged urine is expected, fluids are encouraged to flush the bladder, and CBI is continuous."}, {"id": 126, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "A young adult female is newly prescribed carbamazepine for seizures. Which statement indicates correct understanding?", "o": ["“I will weigh myself every week to check for weight gain.”", "“If I want children, I should not delay starting a family.”", "“I will use condoms as a method of birth control.”", "“A low-sodium diet will need to be followed while on this medication.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Carbamazepine induces liver enzymes and reduces hormonal contraceptive effectiveness, and it’s teratogenic. Also teach to report sore throat/bruising (agranulocytosis)."}, {"id": 127, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "A client receiving epoetin alfa. Which finding indicates a therapeutic response?", "o": ["WBC increased from 3,200 to 6,000/mm³.", "Platelets increased from 115,000 to 145,000/mm³.", "Hemoglobin increased from 10 to 16 g/dL.", "PT increased from 11 to 22 seconds."], "a": 2, "r": "Epoetin stimulates RBC production, so Hgb/Hct rise. (Target Hgb is usually ≤11–12 g/dL; excessive rise increases clot/stroke risk.) WBC suggests filgrastim, platelets suggest oprelvekin."}, {"id": 128, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "IV morphine 1 mg every 2 hours is prescribed. Available: prefilled injectable 2 mg/mL. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Use a 1 mL syringe to remove 1 mg for later use.", "Administer 1 mg and lock up the remaining 1 mg.", "Have another nurse witness the waste of 1 mg from the prefilled injectable prior to administration.", "Administer 1 mg and then have another nurse witness the waste of the remaining medication."], "a": 2, "r": "Controlled substances must be wasted with a witness and documented; never save leftover opioid for later. Follow facility policy on the timing of the waste. (Your copy’s marked answer is C.)"}, {"id": 129, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "A client with heart failure taking digoxin refuses breakfast and reports nausea, weakness, and blurred vision. What should the nurse do first?", "o": ["Obtain current vital sign readings.", "Obtain a prescription for a digoxin level.", "Review the client’s current list of medications.", "Request a prescription for an antiemetic."], "a": 0, "r": "These are signs of digoxin toxicity (also visual halos, bradycardia, arrhythmias). Assess first, especially the apical pulse (hold if <60), then obtain a level and notify the provider."}, {"id": 130, "t": 6, "s": 2, "q": "Which client should the nurse follow up with the PHCP?", "o": ["Cushing’s disease prescribed fludrocortisone.", "Potassium 6.2 mEq/L prescribed sodium polystyrene.", "Aspirin sensitivity prescribed acetaminophen.", "Penicillin rash prescribed an MAOI."], "a": 0, "r": "Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid that retains sodium and water, which worsens the hypertension and fluid retention of Cushing’s disease. It is used in Addison’s disease."}, {"id": 131, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "The nurse is preparing to teach a client with anemia and a new prescription for liquid ferrous sulfate. Which instruction is appropriate?", "o": ["“Take the medication after dinner to avoid daytime drowsiness.”", "“The medication can be taken with milk to prevent GI irritation.”", "“Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids.”", "“Eat plenty of iron-rich foods such as beans and green leafy vegetables.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Dietary iron supports therapy. Iron isn’t sedating, milk decreases absorption, and liquid iron is typically diluted and taken through a straw."}, {"id": 132, "t": 10, "s": 2, "q": "A client receiving a continuous heparin infusion for PE. Which finding indicates an adverse effect?", "o": ["PT 11.6 seconds", "PTT 60 seconds", "INR 3", "Platelet count 90,000/mm³"], "a": 3, "r": "Thrombocytopenia signals heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT): stop heparin and notify the provider. PTT 60 s is in the therapeutic range, and PT/INR monitor warfarin."}, {"id": 133, "t": 0, "s": 2, "q": "A client who had abdominal surgery has these assessment data (PACU 1400 → floor 1415 → reassessment 1430): intraoperative blood loss 600 mL; dressing scant serous drainage; JP drain 5 mL; urine 60→50→40 mL/hr, BP 126/82→120/78→116/68, P 70→82→92, R 16→14→20, T 97.2° F, LR at 150 mL/hr. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Obtain a prescription for a bolus of IV fluids.", "Irrigate the indwelling urinary catheter.", "Continue to monitor the client.", "Place the client on a warming blanket."], "a": 2, "r": "Urine output stays above 30 mL/hr, vitals stay within normal limits, drainage is scant, and temperature is normal, so no intervention is needed. Continue monitoring the trends. (Your copy’s marked answer is C.)"}, {"id": 134, "t": 5, "s": 2, "q": "ABG: pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 38 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 30 mEq/L. Which imbalance?", "o": ["Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"], "a": 1, "r": "High pH with high bicarbonate and normal CO₂ = metabolic alkalosis (normal pH 7.35–7.45, CO₂ 35–45, HCO₃ 22–26)."}, {"id": 135, "t": 6, "s": 2, "q": "A client with type 1 diabetes is taught about exercise. Which statement shows understanding?", "o": ["“I will start my exercise near the time my insulin peaks.”", "“I will eat a carbohydrate snack after exercising if it will be more than 1 hour before my next meal.”", "“I will carry a complex sugar when exercising.”", "“I will avoid exercising if my blood glucose is less than 100.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Exercise lowers glucose for hours; eat a carbohydrate snack to prevent delayed hypoglycemia. Avoid exercising at insulin peak and carry fast-acting simple sugar."}, {"id": 136, "t": 16, "s": 2, "q": "Which statement shows correct understanding of collecting a midstream urine specimen (female client)?", "o": ["“Since I’m right-handed, I’ll use my left hand to spread the labia apart.”", "“I will use the towelette to cleanse using a back-and-forth motion.”", "“I will urinate in the container and then finish urinating in the toilet.”", "“I will hold the specimen container against my skin while collecting.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Spread the labia with the non-dominant hand and keep the dominant hand free for the container. Cleanse front to back, start the stream in the toilet, then collect midstream without touching the container to the skin."}, {"id": 137, "t": 15, "s": 2, "q": "A client with lung cancer receives external radiation. Which statement shows a need for further teaching?", "o": ["“I will use a washcloth with antibacterial soap and water to cleanse the radiation area every day.”", "“I will prevent direct sun exposure during treatment and for at least 1 year.”", "“I should avoid moisturizers, creams, lotions, or powders on the radiation area unless prescribed.”", "“It will be safe for me to be around my pregnant daughter and grandchildren.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Use only mild soap and lukewarm water with the hand (no washcloth, no friction), pat dry, and do not scrub off the markings. External beam radiation does not make the client radioactive."}, {"id": 138, "t": 9, "s": 2, "q": "A client in the ED is bleeding heavily from a deep forearm laceration. After applying direct pressure, which action should the nurse take next?", "o": ["Perform a comprehensive head-to-toe assessment.", "Insert a large-bore IV catheter.", "Cleanse the wound with normal saline.", "Prepare the client for suturing."], "a": 1, "r": "Circulation: control the bleeding, then establish IV access for fluid/blood replacement. Cleaning and suturing happen after hemodynamic stability."}, {"id": 139, "t": 7, "s": 2, "q": "Which manifestations should the nurse monitor for peritonitis in a client receiving peritoneal dialysis?", "o": ["Hyperactive bowel sounds.", "Nausea and vomiting.", "Increased amylase level.", "Burgundy-colored urine."], "a": 1, "r": "Peritonitis: abdominal pain, cloudy outflow, fever, N/V, and decreased bowel sounds. Hyperactive bowel sounds and amylase are not typical."}, {"id": 140, "t": 6, "s": 2, "q": "A client has type 1 diabetes, blood glucose >600, and HHS. Which finding should the nurse expect?", "o": ["Positive urine ketones.", "Fluid volume deficit.", "Serum pH less than 7.35.", "Kussmaul respirations."], "a": 1, "r": "HHS causes profound osmotic diuresis → severe dehydration. Ketosis/acidosis/Kussmaul respirations are DKA findings (HHS has enough insulin to prevent ketones)."}, {"id": 141, "t": 9, "s": 2, "q": "A visitor in the hospital cafeteria begins to cough and signals that they are choking. What should the nurse do first?", "o": ["Begin CPR.", "Call for assistance.", "Prepare to deliver abdominal thrusts.", "Open the airway and attempt to ventilate."], "a": 2, "r": "A conscious choking adult with an obstructed airway needs abdominal thrusts immediately. CPR is for an unresponsive victim."}, {"id": 142, "t": 11, "s": 2, "q": "A client in labor has persistent late decelerations with 50% or more of contractions. The nurse interprets this as", "o": ["uteroplacental insufficiency.", "maternal bradycardia.", "umbilical cord compression.", "fetal head compression."], "a": 0, "r": "Late decels = uteroplacental insufficiency (VELCRO: Variable–cord compression, Early–head compression, Late–placental insufficiency). Turn left, give O₂, stop oxytocin, and notify."}, {"id": 143, "t": 2, "s": 3, "q": "The preceptor is teaching legal principles. Which situation is an example of battery?", "o": ["A nurse finds absent pulses in a casted extremity in the morning and reports it to the PHCP in the afternoon.", "A competent client refuses an antidepressant; the nurse tells them they will be discharged if they do not take it.", "An alert client leaves AMA; the nurse grabs the client’s arm and says they can’t leave.", "A client on a low-sodium diet eats chips; the nurse tells the client wrist restraints will be applied."], "a": 2, "r": "Battery = unwanted physical contact. Grabbing a competent client who has the right to leave is battery (and false imprisonment). A is negligence, B is coercion, and D is assault (threat)."}, {"id": 144, "t": 2, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse should recommend an interdisciplinary conference for the pediatric client who", "o": ["was diagnosed with cystic fibrosis and has a distended abdomen.", "is 10 hours postoperative and just urinated 30 mL of clear yellow urine.", "is receiving hemodialysis with a potassium of 5 mEq/L.", "has norovirus and acute diarrhea."], "a": 0, "r": "Cystic fibrosis is a chronic multisystem disease needing respiratory, nutrition, GI, and PT collaboration. A distended abdomen suggests GI obstruction/malabsorption complications. Other findings are acceptable or short-term."}, {"id": 145, "t": 16, "s": 3, "q": "The preceptor identifies correct care when the newly hired nurse", "o": ["uses sterile gloves when discontinuing a client’s IV infusion.", "empties a client’s colostomy pouch when it is two-thirds full.", "uses gastric pH as an indicator for correct nasogastric tube placement.", "opens the top flap of a sterile package toward the body."], "a": 2, "r": "Aspirate pH ≤5 supports gastric placement (X-ray is the gold standard). Clean gloves are used to remove an IV, pouches are emptied when 1/3 full, and the outer flap of sterile packages is opened away from the body."}, {"id": 146, "t": 2, "s": 3, "q": "A maternity nurse is floated to an adult med-surg unit. It is appropriate to assign the float nurse the client who", "o": ["is scheduled to have an above-the-knee amputation that morning.", "was admitted the night before with COPD.", "is 24 hours postoperative from an appendectomy.", "is scheduled for a cardiac catheterization after admission for chest pain."], "a": 2, "r": "Assign the float nurse clients that match their skills with predictable needs. A stable postoperative client with a common surgery is the safest, and the others require specialty skills or have unstable/complex needs."}, {"id": 147, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "On a pediatric cardiac unit, which client should the nurse initially assess?", "o": ["15-year-old reading with a pulse of 60.", "5-year-old watching TV with a pulse of 78.", "2-year-old playing with toys with a pulse of 68.", "1-month-old sleeping with a pulse of 140."], "a": 2, "r": "A toddler’s normal HR is ~80–140, so 68 is bradycardia. The others are within normal ranges for their ages."}, {"id": 148, "t": 9, "s": 3, "q": "A male client has a full-thickness burn to 35% of the lower body. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has a", "o": ["pulse oximeter reading of 89%.", "hematocrit of 60%.", "temperature of 100.2° F.", "urinary output of 100 mL for the past 2 hours."], "a": 0, "r": "SpO₂ <90–92% indicates hypoxemia (inhalation injury, carbon monoxide) — airway/breathing priority. A high Hct from fluid shifts, a low-grade fever, and 50 mL/hr output are expected."}, {"id": 149, "t": 0, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is prioritizing clients in a mass casualty disaster using the triage tag system. Who should be transported first?", "o": ["Abdominal pain and facial lacerations.", "Burns to 75% of the posterior trunk and lower body.", "Confused with an open wound on the right lower leg.", "Multiple compound fractures and reports chest pain."], "a": 3, "r": "Chest pain suggests possible cardiac/pulmonary injury (fat embolus or contusion), so the client is critical but salvageable. 75% TBSA burns would be expectant in a mass casualty."}, {"id": 150, "t": 1, "s": 3, "q": "A client with dementia is agitated and having difficulty staying in the chair. Which action should the nurse take first?", "o": ["Place a seat alarm on the client’s chair.", "Obtain a prescription to apply a vest restraint.", "Transfer the client to bed and raise 2 side rails.", "Place a table tray in front of the client and across their lap."], "a": 0, "r": "Use the least restrictive alternative first. A seat alarm alerts staff without restricting movement; vests, trays across the lap, and raised rails are restraints."}, {"id": 151, "t": 1, "s": 3, "q": "Which action is appropriate for the nurse to take?", "o": ["Placing a client who had a stem cell transplant on contact precautions.", "Having a client with pertussis wear an N95 during transport.", "Initiating airborne precautions for a client with epiglottitis.", "Wearing an N95 when caring for a client with disseminated varicella zoster."], "a": 3, "r": "Disseminated zoster/varicella requires airborne (N95, negative pressure) plus contact precautions. Pertussis and epiglottitis are droplet, and transplant recipients need protective isolation."}, {"id": 152, "t": 1, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is teaching car seat safety to the parents of a 10-month-old. Which statement shows correct understanding?", "o": ["“We will place the car seat rear-facing in the middle of the back seat.”", "“We will be sure that a hand can fit between the infant and the harness.”", "“Our car seat is a booster seat anchored with the car lap belt.”", "“The harness straps should be below the level of the infant’s armpits.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Infants ride rear-facing in the back seat (center is safest). The harness should be snug (1–2 fingers), and rear-facing harness straps are at or below the shoulders."}, {"id": 153, "t": 10, "s": 3, "q": "Which prescription should the nurse clarify?", "o": ["MI 3 hours ago with a troponin level to be drawn in 3 hours.", "Ischemic stroke symptoms for 2 hours with IV alteplase.", "Pacemaker placed 6 months ago with a chest x-ray scheduled.", "Creatinine 2.8 mg/dL with cardiac catheterization scheduled in the morning."], "a": 3, "r": "Contrast dye is nephrotoxic, and an elevated creatinine indicates impaired kidney function, so clarify/hydrate/hold. The other orders are appropriate (serial troponins; tPA within the window)."}, {"id": 154, "t": 14, "s": 3, "q": "Which action by the nurse is an example of PRIMARY prevention?", "o": ["Providing a community program on stress management.", "Instructing women how to perform monthly breast self-examination.", "Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes and a beginning foot ulcer.", "Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group."], "a": 0, "r": "Primary prevention: health promotion before disease (stress management, vaccines, nutrition). BSE is secondary (screening), and foot care and support groups are tertiary."}, {"id": 155, "t": 14, "s": 3, "q": "It is appropriate for the nurse to tell a group of men that they should", "o": ["begin annual prostate examinations at age 40.", "begin sigmoidoscopy every 10 years at age 50.", "perform monthly testicular self-examination immediately after a bath or shower.", "be tested annually for STDs upon becoming sexually active."], "a": 2, "r": "Warm water relaxes the scrotum, making abnormalities easier to feel. Prostate screening starts at ~50 (or 40–45 if high risk), and colonoscopy is every 10 years from 45–50."}, {"id": 156, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "Which statement by a newly hired nurse about infant/toddler nutrition indicates a need for further teaching?", "o": ["“Milk is introduced at 12 months to an infant who has been breastfed since birth.”", "“Honey can be introduced to an infant at 9 months.”", "“4 oz of fruit juice in a cup can be given to a 13-month-old per day.”", "“Sliced banana is an appropriate snack for a toddler.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Honey is avoided until after 12 months of age due to infant botulism. The other statements are acceptable."}, {"id": 157, "t": 11, "s": 3, "q": "A client 4 hours postpartum has saturated a perineal pad in 10 minutes. What should the nurse do first?", "o": ["Measure the blood pressure.", "Assess for a distended bladder.", "Massage the fundus.", "Administer oxytocin."], "a": 2, "r": "Saturating a pad in 15 minutes is postpartum hemorrhage; boggy uterine atony is the most common cause. Massage the fundus first (it is the first, non-invasive intervention), then empty the bladder, and give oxytocin per order."}, {"id": 158, "t": 13, "s": 3, "q": "A client has right-sided paralysis after a stroke. The son says, “None of this would have happened if I had been there.” What should the nurse say?", "o": ["“It sounds like you feel responsible for what has happened to your mother.”", "“This must be difficult seeing your mother like this, but she will be fine.”", "“Let’s talk about your mother’s therapy plan.”", "“You could not have done anything to prevent this if you had been there.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Reflecting the son’s feelings is therapeutic. Avoid false reassurance, changing the subject, or giving a definitive statement the nurse can’t prove."}, {"id": 159, "t": 13, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is developing a plan of care for a client with histrionic personality disorder. Which intervention is appropriate?", "o": ["Assist the client with appropriate behavior during group therapy sessions.", "Work with the client on ways to develop trusting relationships.", "Communicate with the client using casual language.", "Utilize compassionate behavior to keep the client in control."], "a": 0, "r": "Cluster B clients need structure, firm limits, and coaching on appropriate behavior in groups. Use direct and concrete communication, not casual or vague language."}, {"id": 160, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "In the ED, a school-age child has left arm pain and several bruises on the arms and legs. What should the nurse do first?", "o": ["Separate the child from the parents until abuse is ruled out.", "Obtain a detailed history of how the bruising occurred.", "Report the bruising to Child Protective Services.", "Request a psychiatric consultation."], "a": 1, "r": "Assess first: obtain a detailed, objective history and document. Reporting follows if suspicion remains, and avoid accusing the parents."}, {"id": 161, "t": 7, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke and developed dysphagia. Which statement indicates the need for additional teaching?", "o": ["“Coughing while eating is a sign of dysphagia.”", "“A change in my partner’s voice after swallowing may indicate dysphagia.”", "“I will make sure my partner does not tilt the head forward while eating.”", "“I should monitor for pocketing of food in the mouth.”"], "a": 2, "r": "A chin-tuck (head forward) position closes the airway while swallowing and reduces aspiration. The other statements are correct."}, {"id": 162, "t": 7, "s": 3, "q": "Which statement shows understanding of GERD diet and lifestyle modification?", "o": ["“I will wear clothing with a spandex waistband to support my abdomen.”", "“I will sleep with my head elevated on a wedge-style pillow.”", "“I will sit upright for 30 minutes after meals.”", "“I will have a snack 1 hour before bedtime.”"], "a": 1, "r": "Elevate the head of the bed 6–8 inches (wedge pillow). Avoid tight clothing, sit upright ≥1–2 hours after meals, and avoid eating 2–3 hours before bed."}, {"id": 163, "t": 16, "s": 3, "q": "A client died of pneumonia and an autopsy is anticipated. Which action by the newly hired nurse requires intervention?", "o": ["Asking the family if they wish to participate in bathing the body.", "Removing drainage tubes and IV lines from the body prior to autopsy.", "Removing jewelry and placing it in a bag to return to the family.", "Attaching 2 ID tags to the body and 1 on the outside of the shroud."], "a": 1, "r": "When an autopsy is planned, tubes and lines are left in place. Document and secure valuables, identify the body with 3 tags, and offer family involvement."}, {"id": 164, "t": 7, "s": 3, "q": "A client with atherosclerosis and elevated cholesterol has been taught about diet. Which statement indicates a need for follow-up?", "o": ["“I flavor my fish with lemon juice.”", "“I cook my food with canola oil.”", "“I take an omega-3 supplement daily.”", "“I eat two poached eggs for breakfast every morning.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Egg yolks are high in cholesterol (~185 mg each). Limit them, and use lean protein, fish, and unsaturated oils."}, {"id": 165, "t": 16, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is preparing to insert an IV catheter in an older adult. Which action is appropriate?", "o": ["Shaving the area surrounding the vein.", "Slapping a vein on the back of the hand.", "Placing a washcloth between the client’s skin and the tourniquet.", "Using a gauze dressing with paper tape."], "a": 2, "r": "Older adults have fragile skin and veins. Protect skin from the tourniquet, avoid slapping (hematoma), clip (not shave) hair, and use a transparent or cloth-friendly dressing/tape."}, {"id": 166, "t": 10, "s": 3, "q": "During a PRBC transfusion the client becomes flushed and reports low back pain. After stopping the transfusion, what should the nurse do next?", "o": ["Collect a urine specimen for the lab.", "Notify the blood bank of a possible reaction.", "Start 0.9% sodium chloride through new tubing.", "Administer acetaminophen 500 mg PO."], "a": 2, "r": "Suspected hemolytic reaction: stop the transfusion, keep the IV open with NS via NEW tubing (to avoid infusing residual blood), then notify the provider/blood bank, check vitals, and send samples."}, {"id": 167, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "A 4-year-old is to receive MMR, IPV, and DTaP. Which finding is a contraindication to administering the vaccines?", "o": ["WBC of 3,900/mm³.", "Temperature of 98.8° F.", "Injection-site swelling and redness with previous immunizations.", "Runny nose since yesterday."], "a": 0, "r": "Low WBC suggests possible immunosuppression — live vaccines (MMR) are contraindicated until evaluated. A mild illness, local reactions, and normal temp are not contraindications."}, {"id": 168, "t": 10, "s": 3, "q": "A client is prescribed warfarin. Which lab value indicates a therapeutic response?", "o": ["PT of 15 seconds.", "Platelets increased from 100,000 to 150,000/mm³.", "INR of 2.", "aPTT increased from 30 to 60 seconds."], "a": 2, "r": "Therapeutic INR on warfarin is 2–3. aPTT monitors heparin, and platelets are not a warfarin measure."}, {"id": 169, "t": 16, "s": 3, "q": "Which action is appropriate when preparing an IM injection for an adult?", "o": ["Limit the medication to 4 mL when using the deltoid muscle.", "Hold the skin taut when using the Z-track method.", "Use the deltoid site for a client who needs 3 mL.", "Draw up an air bubble after the medication has been prepared."], "a": 1, "r": "Z-track: pull the skin laterally (taut) to seal the medication in the muscle. The deltoid is limited to 1 mL (ventrogluteal up to 3 mL), and the air lock is optional per policy."}, {"id": 170, "t": 10, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse should follow up with the PHCP for a client who has", "o": ["constipation-predominant IBS and has been prescribed loperamide.", "diabetes mellitus, a CT scan with IV contrast, and a prescription to withhold metformin.", "a prosthetic mitral valve and INR 3.0 on warfarin.", "MRSA infection, an allergy to sulfonamides, and has been prescribed vancomycin."], "a": 0, "r": "Loperamide slows motility and is contraindicated in constipation. Withholding metformin around contrast is correct, INR 3 is therapeutic for mitral prosthetic valves, and vancomycin isn’t a sulfonamide."}, {"id": 171, "t": 7, "s": 3, "q": "A client with PUD in the ED has BP 92/58 mm Hg (history of PUD, vomiting). What is a priority?", "o": ["Initiate an IV nitroglycerin drip.", "Administer an IV antiemetic.", "Determine if the client has any stressors.", "Type and cross for a PRBC transfusion."], "a": 3, "r": "Hypotension in PUD suggests GI hemorrhage and hypovolemia, so prepare for transfusion (type and cross), start IV fluids, and notify the provider. Nitroglycerin would worsen hypotension."}, {"id": 172, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "A child is postoperative from a VP shunt placement and has become difficult to arouse. Which action should the nurse take immediately?", "o": ["Measure the head circumference.", "Turn the client to the operative side.", "Elevate the head of the bed to 30 degrees.", "Prepare for endotracheal tube insertion."], "a": 2, "r": "Decreased LOC suggests shunt malfunction with increased ICP. Use the least invasive intervention first (raise HOB 30°) and notify the provider. Do not lie on the shunt side."}, {"id": 173, "t": 9, "s": 3, "q": "A client in hypovolemic shock has a pulse of 116. IV 0.9% NaCl has been initiated. What should the nurse do next?", "o": ["Initiate oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula.", "Obtain a prescription for a vasodilator.", "Document and continue to monitor.", "Insert an indwelling urethral catheter."], "a": 0, "r": "Shock decreases oxygen delivery, so start oxygen, along with IV fluids. Vasodilators would worsen hypotension, and the client needs intervention, not just monitoring."}, {"id": 174, "t": 5, "s": 3, "q": "Which ABG results should the nurse expect in a client with DKA?", "o": ["pH 7.24, PaCO₂ 50, HCO₃ 24", "pH 7.47, PaCO₂ 38, HCO₃ 32", "pH 7.36, PaCO₂ 42, HCO₃ 26", "pH 7.25, PaCO₂ 35, HCO₃ 14"], "a": 3, "r": "DKA produces metabolic acidosis: low pH and low bicarbonate, with compensatory (low-normal) CO₂ from Kussmaul breathing."}, {"id": 175, "t": 6, "s": 3, "q": "A client with Addison disease is at risk for Addisonian crisis. Which action should the nurse take?", "o": ["Provide a low-carbohydrate diet.", "Monitor blood pressure frequently.", "Administer oral desmopressin.", "Restrict fluid intake."], "a": 1, "r": "Adrenal crisis causes severe hypotension, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia. Monitor BP frequently, replace glucocorticoids/fluids, and prevent stress triggers."}, {"id": 176, "t": 5, "s": 3, "q": "A client who had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has", "o": ["a fluid intake of 1,000 mL and urine output of 800 mL over 8 hours.", "a weight increase from 199 to 201 lb over 72 hours.", "a WBC decrease from 6,000 to 5,000/mm³.", "a temperature increase from 99.5 to 100.3° F over 4 hours."], "a": 3, "r": "Immunosuppressed clients may show only subtle signs of infection or rejection; a rising temperature must be reported. Other findings are within acceptable limits."}, {"id": 177, "t": 4, "s": 3, "q": "A client on mechanical ventilation has a high-pressure alarm. The nurse has checked for potential causes and cannot determine the problem. What should the nurse do next?", "o": ["Manually ventilate the client with a bag-mask-valve device.", "Call respiratory therapy to check the ventilator settings.", "Shut the alarm off and call a code.", "Assess the client’s lung sounds and breathing pattern."], "a": 0, "r": "If the cause cannot be found quickly, disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with 100% O₂ to ensure oxygenation while help is called. The assessment was already done."}, {"id": 178, "t": 3, "s": 3, "q": "A client is to receive a permanent pacemaker. Which statement should the nurse include in the teaching?", "o": ["“You will need to avoid using cellular phones after the pacemaker is placed.”", "“Swelling and redness of the operative area are normal for the first 2 days.”", "“Avoid lifting the arm above the head on the pacemaker side for the next 4 weeks.”", "“Keep your incision covered for the first 4 days.”"], "a": 2, "r": "Limit arm movement/raising above the shoulder until the leads are secured (about 4–6 weeks). Cell phones are fine at a distance of ~6 inches, and redness/swelling should be reported."}, {"id": 179, "t": 1, "s": 3, "q": "Which client should be placed in a private room when admitting several clients? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["A client with inhalation anthrax diagnosed 48 hours ago.", "A client with a fever of unknown origin.", "A client diagnosed with HIV 2 weeks ago.", "A client with a WBC of 2,400/mm³."], "a": 3, "r": "A WBC of 2,400 is leukopenia/neutropenia and needs protection from infection (private/protective room). Inhalation anthrax and HIV require standard precautions only."}, {"id": 180, "t": 14, "s": 3, "q": "Which action by the nurse is an example of SECONDARY prevention? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group.", "Performing monthly self-breast examinations.", "Providing a community program on stress reduction.", "Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes."], "a": 1, "r": "Secondary prevention = early detection/screening (BSE, mammograms, labs). Stress reduction is primary, and foot care/support groups prevent progression (tertiary)."}, {"id": 181, "t": 11, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is caring for a pregnant client whose LMP began October 21. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the due date? (Exam 1 recording — options reconstructed)", "o": ["July 28", "July 14", "August 28", "January 28"], "a": 0, "r": "Add 7 days: Oct 21 → Oct 28, subtract 3 months → July 28."}, {"id": 182, "t": 13, "s": 3, "q": "A client has paranoid schizophrenia and a history of auditory hallucinations. The client is yelling and pacing in the dayroom, “I will do it.” What should the nurse do? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Obtain increased supervision for the client.", "Provide the client with a diverting activity.", "Send the client to their room."], "a": 0, "r": "“I will do it” may be a command hallucination to harm self/others. Increase supervision for safety; you cannot divert a client actively responding to hallucinations."}, {"id": 183, "t": 13, "s": 3, "q": "A client in the manic phase of bipolar disorder says, “I am bored.” Which activity should the nurse suggest? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Walking with the nurse in the courtyard.", "Basketball.", "Participating in group."], "a": 0, "r": "Manic clients need low-stimulation, non-competitive, solitary or one-on-one activity to burn off energy. Competitive games and groups increase stimulation."}, {"id": 184, "t": 7, "s": 3, "q": "Which statement by a client who recently had a colostomy placed indicates a correct understanding of diet teaching? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["“Eating yogurt can help decrease the amount of gas I have.”", "“I should eliminate pasta from my diet so I don’t develop loose stools.”", "“Drinking carbonated beverages can help control odor.”", "“My largest meal of the day should be in the evening.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Yogurt/buttermilk help decrease gas and odor. Carbonated drinks increase gas, large evening meals fill the pouch overnight, and pasta is not usually restricted."}, {"id": 185, "t": 8, "s": 3, "q": "A client who recently received hearing aids. Which statement indicates the need for ADDITIONAL teaching? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["“I will clean the hearing aids with alcohol wipes.”", "“I expect the hearing aids to whistle when I cup my hand over them.”", "“I will avoid using hair spray.”", "“I will clean my hearing aids with soap and water.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Alcohol damages the device. Clean with a dry cloth (or a slightly damp cloth with mild soap and water — keep the device dry). Whistling when cupped is expected feedback."}, {"id": 186, "t": 4, "s": 3, "q": "A client with asthma is prescribed beclomethasone. Which statement indicates a correct understanding? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["“I should rinse my mouth out after this medication.”", "“I should use this medication to treat my symptoms during an asthma attack.”", "“I need to check my pulse for 1 full minute.”", "“I need to watch my caffeine intake.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Beclomethasone is an inhaled corticosteroid: a controller medication, not a rescue inhaler. Rinse and spit to prevent oral thrush."}, {"id": 187, "t": 10, "s": 3, "q": "A client is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which finding indicates a side effect? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Respiratory rate of 10/min.", "Elevated calcium level.", "Urinary output of 125 mL/4 hours.", "BP change from 122/74 to 136/84."], "a": 0, "r": "Magnesium is a CNS depressant; toxicity causes respiratory depression, loss of deep tendon reflexes, and low urine output. The antidote is calcium gluconate. Note that 125 mL/4 hours (≈31 mL/hr) is borderline acceptable."}, {"id": 188, "t": 5, "s": 3, "q": "A client who had an open radical prostatectomy was discharged with a urinary catheter. Which statement requires follow-up? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["“I will not be able to take a shower for the next 2 weeks.”", "“I will wash my catheter once a day with soap and water.”", "“I will change my catheter drainage bag at least once a week or as needed.”", "“I will use a leg bag with a catheter.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Showering is allowed with a catheter; the bag stays below the bladder. Daily soap-and-water cleaning, weekly bag changes, and leg bags are appropriate."}, {"id": 189, "t": 4, "s": 3, "q": "The nurse is monitoring a client following a thoracentesis. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client develops (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["a heart rate increase from 95 to 110 bpm.", "an oxygen saturation decrease from 99% to 96%.", "vesicular breath sounds in the peripheral lung field.", "serosanguineous drainage from the puncture site."], "a": 0, "r": "A rising heart rate may indicate pneumothorax or bleeding/tension. Sats of 96% are normal, vesicular breath sounds are expected, and slight serosanguineous drainage is expected."}, {"id": 190, "t": 9, "s": 3, "q": "A client in hypovolemic shock has a heart rate of 160 bpm and is receiving oxygen at 2 L via nasal cannula. Which action is the priority? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Obtain a prescription to increase the oxygen to 4 L/min.", "Document the findings and continue to monitor.", "Notify the PHCP.", "Administer the prescribed normal saline at 150 mL/hr."], "a": 3, "r": "Hypovolemic shock needs volume replacement. Give the prescribed isotonic IV fluid right away, then notify the provider."}, {"id": 191, "t": 12, "s": 3, "q": "A 3-year-old weighing 33 lb had 160 mL of urine in 8 hours. What action should the nurse take? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Notify the PHCP.", "Provide oral fluids.", "Perform a bladder scan at the bedside.", "Continue to monitor."], "a": 3, "r": "33 lb = 15 kg. 160 mL ÷ 8 hr = 20 mL/hr ≈ 1.3 mL/kg/hr, which is adequate (≥1 mL/kg/hr in children), so it is expected; keep monitoring."}, {"id": 192, "t": 9, "s": 3, "q": "A client has developed evisceration after abdominal surgery. After calling for assistance, which action should the nurse take next? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Place the client in a supine position with the knees drawn toward the abdomen.", "Use a moist ABD pad to cover the abdomen."], "a": 1, "r": "Evisceration: call for help, cover the viscera with a sterile saline-moistened dressing (keeps the organs from drying), position low Fowler’s with knees bent to reduce abdominal tension, and notify the surgeon."}, {"id": 193, "t": 9, "s": 3, "q": "The PACU nurse assesses a client who had a below-the-knee amputation. What should the nurse prioritize assessing for? (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["Hypovolemic shock from bleeding.", "Neurogenic shock."], "a": 0, "r": "Major bleeding is the earliest, most life-threatening complication after amputation, so check the residual limb dressing and vital signs; keep a tourniquet at the bedside."}, {"id": 194, "t": 8, "s": 3, "q": "A client had lumbar spinal surgery 8 hours ago. It is a priority to notify the surgeon if the client (Exam 1 recording)", "o": ["independently log-rolls in bed.", "has 125 mL serosanguineous fluid in the Hemovac.", "has a moderate amount of clear drainage on the surgical dressing."], "a": 2, "r": "Clear (halo-ring/glucose-positive) drainage suggests a cerebrospinal fluid leak, which risks meningitis and requires immediate reporting. Log-rolling and serosanguineous drainage are expected."}, {"id": 195, "t": 3, "s": 3, "q": "A client is on bed rest. Which action will help prevent pulmonary embolism? (Exam 1 recording — one option missing in source)", "o": ["Determine if the client is using the incentive spirometer.", "Instruct the client to perform leg exercises.", "Elevate the client’s legs on a pillow."], "a": 1, "r": "Leg exercises/ROM prevent venous stasis and DVT (a PE source). Incentive spirometry prevents atelectasis/pneumonia, and pillows under the knees can impair venous return."}, {"id": 196, "t": 1, "s": 3, "q": "Which sequence correctly describes removal (doffing) of personal protective equipment? (Exam 1 recording — options reconstructed)", "o": ["Gloves, goggles, gown, mask", "Mask, gown, goggles, gloves", "Gown, gloves, mask, goggles", "Goggles, mask, gloves, gown"], "a": 0, "r": "Remove the most contaminated items first: gloves → goggles/face shield → gown → mask (mask last, outside the room), followed by hand hygiene."}, {"id": 197, "t": 0, "s": 4, "q": "Which of the following clients should the nurse delegate the taking of vital signs for to the unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP)?", "o": ["A client being prepared for elective facial surgery with a history of stable hypertension.", "A client receiving a blood transfusion with a history of transfusion reactions.", "A client recently started on a new antiarrhythmic agent.", "A client who is admitted frequently with asthma attacks."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: “A client being prepared for elective facial surgery with a history of…” – The client is stable.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A client receiving a blood transfusion with a history of transfusion…” – This client is unstable.\n• “A client recently started on a new antiarrhythmic agent” – The UAP cannot take apical pulses.\n• “A client who is admitted frequently with asthma attacks” – This client is unstable and vital sign measurement should not be delegated.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 198, "t": 1, "s": 5, "q": "Which of the following medical asepsis actions should the nurse take to reduce and prevent the spread of microorganisms? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Practicing hand hygiene.", "Reapplying a sterile dressing.", "Sterilizing contaminating items.", "Applying a sterile gown and gloves.", "Routinely cleaning the hospital environment.", "Wearing clean gloves to prevent direct contact with blood or body fluids."], "a": [0, 4, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Practicing hand hygiene” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n• “Routinely cleaning the hospital environment” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n• “Wearing clean gloves to prevent direct contact with blood or body flu…” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Reapplying a sterile dressing” – This is a sterile procedure.\n• “Sterilizing contaminating items” – This is a sterile procedure.\n• “Applying a sterile gown and gloves” – This is a sterile procedure.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 199, "t": 1, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with a decubitus ulcer (stage 4). The wound culture result shows MRSA in the wound bed. Which type of transmission-based precautions should the nurse institute?", "o": ["Standard.", "Droplet.", "Contact.", "Airborne."], "a": 2, "r": "Correct: “Contact” – MRSA in a wound indicates contact isolation.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Standard” – Although they are used for all clients, standard precautions would not help decrease the spread of MRSA.\n• “Droplet” – MRSA in the nares would indicate droplet isolation.\n• “Airborne” – This isolation is indicated for very small molecules that linger for long periods in the air.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 200, "t": 0, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse is triaging clients involved in a mass causality disaster using the color-coded tagging system. Which clients should be tagged red? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Is deceased.", "Has chest pain.", "Has a leg sprain.", "Has a chest wound.", "Has multiple closed fractures.", "Has full thickness burns over 30% of the body."], "a": [1, 3, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Has chest pain” – This client would be in the red category.\n• “Has a chest wound” – This client would be in the red category.\n• “Has full thickness burns over 30% of the body” – This client would be red since they have less then 65% burns on their body.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Is deceased” – This client would be in the black category.\n• “Has a leg sprain” – This client would be in the green category.\n• “Has multiple closed fractures” – This client would be in the yellow category.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 201, "t": 1, "s": 5, "q": "The charge nurse observes a newly hired nurse caring for a client with a wrist restraint. Which of the following observations by the charge nurse requires follow up with the newly hired nurse?", "o": ["A safety knot was used to secure the restraint.", "The call light was placed within reach of the client.", "The restraint was applied tightly around the client's wrist.", "The clients record indicates that the restraint was released every 2 hours."], "a": 2, "r": "Correct: “The restraint was applied tightly around the client's wrist” – The client's restraints should not be tight.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A safety knot was used to secure the restraint” – This is the correct type of knot to use.\n• “The call light was placed within reach of the client” – This is correct for safety.\n• “The clients record indicates that the restraint was released every 2…” – This is the correct amount of time that restraints should be released for ROM.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 202, "t": 1, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse is discussing accident prevention with the family of an older client who is being discharged following hip surgery. Which of the following physical factors place the client at risk for injury in the home? Select all that apply.", "o": ["A night light in the bathroom.", "Elevated toilet seat with armrests.", "Cooking equipment (ex. stove).", "Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors.", "A low thermostat setting on the water heater.", "Common household objects such as a doormat or rug."], "a": [2, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Cooking equipment (ex. stove)” – The client could burn themselves if they fall.\n• “Common household objects such as a doormat or rug” – The client could fall.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A night light in the bathroom” – This is safe.\n• “Elevated toilet seat with armrests” – This is safe.\n• “Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors” – This is safe.\n• “A low thermostat setting on the water heater” – This is safe.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 203, "t": 2, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse preceptor is describing critical pathways and variance analysis to a new graduate. The preceptor explains that a variance analysis is preformed on all clients with respect to which time frame?", "o": ["Continuously.", "Daily.", "Every third day.", "Every other day."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: “Continuously” – It is an ongoing process.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Daily”\n• “Every third day”\n• “Every other day”\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 204, "t": 3, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse is teaching a client with cardiomyopathy about safety measures when at home. Which of the following instructions is most important?", "o": ["Reporting pain.", "Taking a vasodilator.", "Avoid over the counter medications.", "Moving slowly from a sitting to a standing position."], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: “Moving slowly from a sitting to a standing position” – Moving fast could result in orthostatic hypotension and the client would fall.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Reporting pain” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n• “Taking a vasodilator” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n• “Avoid over the counter medications” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 205, "t": 0, "s": 5, "q": "The nurse is assigning clients to a LPN/LVN and UAP. Which clients should the nurse assign to the LPN/LVN? Select all that apply", "o": ["A client needing frequent suctioning.", "A client requiring a dressing change to the foot.", "A client requiring a dressing change to the foot.", "A client requiring a dressing change to the foot.", "A client requiring a dressing change to the foot."], "a": [0, 1, 3], "r": "Correct:\n• “A client needing frequent suctioning” – This is under the LPN scope of practice.\n• “A client requiring a dressing change to the foot” – This is under the LPN scope of practice.\n• “A client requiring a dressing change to the foot” – This is under the LPN scope of practice.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A client requiring a dressing change to the foot” – This can be done by the UAP.\n• “A client requiring a dressing change to the foot” – This can be done by the UAP.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 206, "t": 9, "s": 5, "q": "Which of the following actions should the nurse take when caring for a client with accidental poisoning? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Save vomitus for laboratory analysis.", "Place the client in a supine position.", "Induce vomiting if a household cleaner was ingested.", "Assess for airway patency, breathing, and circulation.", "Determine the type and amount of substance ingested.", "Remove any visible materials from the nose and mouth."], "a": [0, 3, 4, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Save vomitus for laboratory analysis” – This is correct.\n• “Assess for airway patency, breathing, and circulation” – This is correct.\n• “Determine the type and amount of substance ingested” – This is correct.\n• “Remove any visible materials from the nose and mouth” – This is correct.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Place the client in a supine position” – This could result in aspiration pneumonia.\n• “Induce vomiting if a household cleaner was ingested” – This would cause the esophagus to suffer burns twice.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 207, "t": 10, "s": 6, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with massive hemorrhage. The primary healthcare provider (PHCP) prescribes 1 unit of whole blood to be administered immediately. Which of the following can the nurse safety administer to this client?", "o": ["A negative.", "B negative.", "AB negative.", "O negative."], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: “O negative” – This type of blood is the universal donor.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n• “B negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n• “AB negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 208, "t": 10, "s": 6, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client receiving packed red blood cells(PRBCs). Which of the following manifestations should alert the nurse to a hemolytic transfusion reaction? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Headache.", "Tachycardia.", "Hypertension.", "Apprehension.", "Distended neck veins.", "Sense of impending doom."], "a": [0, 1, 3, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Headache”\n• “Tachycardia”\n• “Apprehension”\n• “Sense of impending doom”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hypertension” – The client would have hypotension.\n• “Distended neck veins” – This is circulatory overload reaction.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 209, "t": 10, "s": 6, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client prescribed a blood transfusion. The client asks if any disease can be transmitted from the donor. Which of the following diseases can be transmitted from a donor? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Epstein-Barr.", "HIV.", "CMV.", "Hepatitis A.", "Malaria."], "a": [0, 1, 2], "r": "Correct:\n• “Epstein-Barr” – Transmitted by blood route.\n• “HIV” – Transmitted by blood route.\n• “CMV” – Transmitted by blood route.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hepatitis A” – Transmitted by oral-fecal route.\n• “Malaria” – Transmitted by mosquitoes.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 210, "t": 10, "s": 6, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client prescribed 2 units of packed red blood cells. Which of the following actions should the nurse implement when caring for the client. Select all that apply.", "o": ["Verify that the ABO and Rh of the 2 units are the same.", "Infuse a unit of PRBCs in less than 4 hours.", "Stop the transfusion if a reaction occurs but keep the line open.", "Take the vital signs every 15 minutes while the unit is transfusing.", "Inspect the blood bag for leaks, abnormal color, and clots.", "Use a 22-gauge catheter for optimal flow of a blood transfusion."], "a": [1, 2, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• “Infuse a unit of PRBCs in less than 4 hours” – A unit should infuse in 4 hours or less.\n• “Stop the transfusion if a reaction occurs but keep the line open” – If a reaction occurs you should stop the infusion and KVO.\n• “Inspect the blood bag for leaks, abnormal color, and clots” – Blood should be inspected for these prior to administration.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Verify that the ABO and Rh of the 2 units are the same” – You need to verify units with the client's identifier information not the bags.\n• “Take the vital signs every 15 minutes while the unit is transfusing” – Vitals should be taken prior to administration, every 15 minutes for the first hour, then every hour until complete. Not every 15 minutes for the entire transfusion.\n• “Use a 22-gauge catheter for optimal flow of a blood transfusion” – A 19 gauge or larger catheter should be used so RBCs are not destroyed by the lumen.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 211, "t": 0, "s": 7, "q": "Which of the following activities should the nurse delegate to the unlicensed assistant personnel(UAP)? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Take vital signs on a new admission.", "Assist with the narcotic count.", "Check the crash cart for necessary supplies.", "Administer a tap water enema.", "Obtain client finger sticks on diabetic clients.", "Demonstrate how to use an incentive spirometry."], "a": [3, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• “Administer a tap water enema” – There are no medications or electrolytes in the enema.\n• “Obtain client finger sticks on diabetic clients” – This is in their scope of practice.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Take vital signs on a new admission” – Only RNs assess new admissions.\n• “Assist with the narcotic count” – Only nurse can do this.\n• “Check the crash cart for necessary supplies” – Only nurse can do this.\n• “Demonstrate how to use an incentive spirometry” – They can not teach.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 212, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "Which of the following clinical situations should be viewed as an assault?", "o": ["The nurse threatens to apply restraints to a client who is exhibiting aggressive behavior.", "The client requests a medical discharge, but the nurse physically forces the client to stay.", "The charge nurse sends an email to a staff member which includes a poor performance evaluation about another person.", "The nurse overhears the primary health care provider making derogatory remarks to the client about the nurse's level of competency."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: “The nurse threatens to apply restraints to a client who is exhibiting…” – Assault is a threat to harm.\n\nNot correct:\n• “The client requests a medical discharge, but the nurse physically for…” – This would be battery.\n• “The charge nurse sends an email to a staff member which includes a po…” – This would be inappropriate and violate the staff members rights.\n• “The nurse overhears the primary health care provider making derogator…” – This would be defamation.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 213, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "Which of the following actions by the nurse demonstrate ethical practice? Select all that apply", "o": ["Notifies PHCP about a client refusing dialysis and wants to stop.", "Educates client about use and benefits of incentive spirometry when refusing to use device.", "Refuses to stay overnight on a mandated shift due to fatigue, leaves at the end of current scheduled shift.", "Confronts a coworker who took narcotics out of a client's medication bin for personal use and takes no further action.", "Asks a client to “take a walk down the hall so their roommate can speak with the PHCP privately about their biopsy results.”", "Tells a client they will find out an answer for them."], "a": [0, 1, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Notifies PHCP about a client refusing dialysis and wants to stop”\n• “Educates client about use and benefits of incentive spirometry when r…”\n• “Tells a client they will find out an answer for them”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Refuses to stay overnight on a mandated shift due to fatigue, leaves…”\n• “Confronts a coworker who took narcotics out of a client's medication…”\n• “Asks a client to “take a walk down the hall so their roommate can spe…”\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 214, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "The nurse is caring for a multiple gunshot victim. Which of the following actions should the nurse take to properly secure and handle legal evidence? Select all that apply. Place bags on the hands and feet.", "o": ["Place paper bags on the hands and feet", "Give clothing and wallet to the family.", "Cut clothing along the seams, avoiding bullet holes.", "Collect all personal items, including items from clothing pockets.", "Place wet clothing and personal belongings in a labeled, sealed plastic bag.", "Do not allow family members, significant others, or friends to be alone with the client."], "a": [0, 2, 3, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Place paper bags on the hands and feet” – Need to preserve evidence.\n• “Cut clothing along the seams, avoiding bullet holes” – Forensics will need to test the bullet hole areas.\n• “Collect all personal items, including items from clothing pockets” – It will all need listed for potential evidence.\n• “Do not allow family members, significant others, or friends to be alo…” – They could tamper with evidence.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Give clothing and wallet to the family” – This might be needed for evidence in future.\n• “Place wet clothing and personal belongings in a labeled, sealed plast…” – This could harm the evidence if placed with wet items. They should be placed separately, labeled, and sealed.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 215, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Which of the following are behaviors of active listening? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Maintaining an open posture.", "Writing down what the client says so that details are not forgotten.", "Establishing and maintaining eye contact.", "Nodding in agreement with the client throughout the conversation.", "Responding positively when giving feedback."], "a": [0, 2, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• “Maintaining an open posture”\n• “Establishing and maintaining eye contact”\n• “Responding positively when giving feedback”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Writing down what the client says so that details are not forgotten” – The nurse should give the client undivided attention.\n• “Nodding in agreement with the client throughout the conversation” – The nurse should give quality verbal feedback.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 216, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Which of the following clients is considered the most at risk for the onset of stress related problems?", "o": ["Client whose father died 3 months ago and lost their job.", "Client graduating from college and getting married in a month.", "Client whose new business is growing slowly financially.", "Client passed over for promotion, who then quit and started their own business."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: “Client whose father died 3 months ago and lost their job” – They have suffered 2 significant loses.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Client graduating from college and getting married in a month” – These are positive events.\n• “Client whose new business is growing slowly financially” – This is considered a positive event.\n• “Client passed over for promotion, who then quit and started their own…” – Only 1 significant loss.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 217, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Hospital-based admission has become more oriented to crisis intervention. Which of the following clients have priority for admission to an acute care facility? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Client who lives alone.", "Client who is acutely psychotic.", "Client who is acutely psychotic.", "Client who is not sleeping and has a lack of appetite.", "Client who is depressed.", "Clients who is not complying with medication regimens"], "a": [1, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• “Client who is acutely psychotic” – Safety issues is the admission criteria for mental health clients.\n• “Client who is depressed” – Safety issues is the admission criteria for mental health clients.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Client who lives alone” – This client is not at a safety risk.\n• “Client who is acutely psychotic” – This client is not at a safety risk.\n• “Client who is not sleeping and has a lack of appetite” – This client is not at a  safety risk.\n• “Clients who is not complying with medication regimens” – This client is not at a safety risk.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 218, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Which of the following foods should the nurse eliminate from the diet of a client experiencing alcohol withdrawal?", "o": ["Milk.", "Regular coffee.", "Orange juice.", "Eggs."], "a": 1, "r": "Correct: “Regular coffee” – Coffee contains caffeine that increases tremors and agitation.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Milk” – Milk is an appropriate choice.\n• “Orange juice” – Orange juice is an appropriate choice.\n• “Eggs” – Eggs are an appropriate choice.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 219, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Which of the following clients are at highest risk for suicide completion?", "o": ["85-year-old Caucasian male who lives alone after his wife's death last month.", "34-year-old single Hispanic female who recently was diagnosed with cancer.", "15-year-old African American female whose boyfriend broke up with her several days ago.", "52-year-old Asian male who was terminated from his job because of downsizing."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: “85-year-old Caucasian male who lives alone after his wife's death las…” – Over age 65, male and Caucasian are associated with high suicidal rates.\n\nNot correct:\n• “34-year-old single Hispanic female who recently was diagnosed with ca…” – Client is female and Hispanic. Both are associated with low suicidal rates.\n• “15-year-old African American female whose boyfriend broke up with her…” – Client is female and African American. Both are associated with low suicidal rates.\n• “52-year-old Asian male who was terminated from his job because of dow…” – Although client is male the  Asian culture does not have a high suicidal rate.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 220, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with a lithium level of 1.8 mEq/L. The client is due for the next scheduled dose of lithium. Which of the following actions should the nurse perform? Hold the dose of lithium.", "o": ["Administer the dose of lithium.", "Hold the 5PM dose of lithium", "Notify the clients family.", "Obtain a set of vital signs."], "a": 1, "r": "Correct: “Hold the 5PM dose of lithium” – The level is above 1.5.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administer the dose of lithium” – The lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n• “Notify the clients family” – The lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n• “Obtain a set of vital signs” – The lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 221, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "The primary health care provider prescribes risperidone for a client with Alzheimer's disease. The nurse anticipates administering this medication to help decrease which of the following behaviors? Confusion and withdrawal", "o": ["Sleep disturbances", "Concomitant depression", "Agitation and assault potential"], "a": 2, "r": "Correct: “Agitation and assault potential” – Antipsychotics are most effective with agitation and assault potential. Confusion and withdrawal No, the medication is an antipsychotic.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Sleep disturbances” – The medication is an antipsychotic.\n• “Concomitant depression” – The medication is an antipsychotic.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 222, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client with borderline personality disorder. The client tells the nurse “you are the best nurse here; the others just want a paycheck.” Which of the following responses by the nurse is appropriate?", "o": ["“Thank you, you are a nice person.”", "“All of the nurses here provide good care.”", "“Other clients have told me that also.”", "“Have you had problems with other nurses?”"], "a": 1, "r": "Correct: ““All of the nurses here provide good care.”” – This stops the client from ‘splitting’ the nurses.\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Thank you, you are a nice person.”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n• ““Other clients have told me that also.”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n• ““Have you had problems with other nurses?”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 223, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "Which of the following medication amounts is appropriate for a client who is being treated with imipramine on an outpatient basis for recurring depression and suicidal ideation to have prescribed?", "o": ["30-day supply", "21-day supply", "14-day supply", "7-day supply"], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: “7-day supply” – This medication amount would not allow the client to overdose if taken in its entirety.\n\nNot correct:\n• “30-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 30-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n• “21-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 21-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n• “14-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 14-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 224, "t": 13, "s": 8, "q": "A paranoid client reports that men in blue clothing keep looking in the window and whispering. Which of the following responses by the nurse is most appropriate?", "o": ["“Those men are groundskeepers. They are talking about their work, not you.”", "“Do not take things personally, not everyone who is whispering is whispering about you.”", "“Do not pay attention to the men. Shall we play cards instead.”", "“I will close the drapes so you can't see the men.”"], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: ““Those men are groundskeepers. They are talking about their work, not…” – The nurse does not want to agree with the client. They should be truthful and matter of fact.\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Do not take things personally, not everyone who is whispering is whi…” – This disregards the clients thought and appears condescending.\n• ““Do not pay attention to the men. Shall we play cards instead.”” – This is distraction and the client will still have the same thoughts.\n• ““I will close the drapes so you can't see the men.”” – This does not address the false thoughts of the client.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 225, "t": 5, "s": 9, "q": "A client has a total serum calcium level of 7.6mg/dL. Which clinical manifestations should the nurse expect to note on assessment of the client? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Constipation.", "Muscle twitching.", "Hypoactive bowel sounds.", "Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes.", "Positive Trousseau and Chvostek sign.", "Prolonged ST interval and QT interval on EKG."], "a": [1, 3, 4, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Muscle twitching” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Positive Trousseau and Chvostek sign” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Prolonged ST interval and QT interval on EKG” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Constipation” – Client would have diarrhea.\n• “Hypoactive bowel sounds” – Bowel sounds would be hyperactive.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 226, "t": 11, "s": 10, "q": "A female client tells the nurse that she is quite sure she is pregnant. Which of the following is a positive sign of pregnancy?", "o": ["Amenorrhea.", "Uterine enlargement.", "HCG detected in the urine.", "Fetal heartbeat."], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: “Fetal heartbeat” – This is a positive sign.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Amenorrhea” – This is a presumptive sign.\n• “Uterine enlargement” – This is a probable sign.\n• “HCG detected in the urine” – This is a probable sign.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 227, "t": 11, "s": 10, "q": "The nurse is assessing a pregnant client with a diagnosis of abruptio placentae. Which of the following should the nurse expect to assess on the client? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Uterine irritability.", "Uterine tenderness.", "Bright red vaginal bleeding.", "Abdominal and lower back pain.", "Strong and frequent contractions.", "Nonreassuring fetal heart patterns."], "a": [0, 1, 3, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “Uterine irritability”\n• “Uterine tenderness”\n• “Abdominal and lower back pain”\n• “Nonreassuring fetal heart patterns”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Bright red vaginal bleeding” – Dark red bleeding.\n• “Strong and frequent contractions” – Frequent low intensity contractions.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 228, "t": 14, "s": 11, "q": "The nurse is performing an assessment on an older adult client. Which manifestations are considered normal age-related changes in the eye? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Clear Lens.", "Blurred vision.", "Protruding cornea.", "Increased tear production.", "Diminished pupillary adaptation to darkness.", "Increased ability to discriminate among colors."], "a": [1, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• “Blurred vision” – Vision becomes blurry with age progression.\n• “Diminished pupillary adaptation to darkness” – This is a normal age-related change.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Clear Lens” – The lens becomes cloudy.\n• “Protruding cornea” – This is not a normal age-related change.\n• “Increased tear production” – They have a decreased tear production.\n• “Increased ability to discriminate among colors” – They have a decreased ability to discriminate among colors.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 229, "t": 14, "s": 11, "q": "The nurse is working at an osteoporosis screening clinic. Which of the following female clients is at greatest risk for developing osteoporosis? Select all that apply.", "o": ["An Asian female.", "A large framed, dark-skinned female.", "A female who started menopause early.", "A female with a family history of osteoporosis.", "A female who has a physically active lifestyle.", "A female with a family history of osteoporosis."], "a": [0, 2, 3, 5], "r": "Correct:\n• “An Asian female” – Asian and Caucasian females are prone to osteoporosis.\n• “A female who started menopause early” – Menopause predisposes females to osteoporosis.\n• “A female with a family history of osteoporosis” – Genetics predisposes females to osteoporosis.\n• “A female with a family history of osteoporosis” – Calcium and vitamin D deficiency are associated with osteoporosis.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A large framed, dark-skinned female” – Its common in small framed females.\n• “A female who has a physically active lifestyle” – Those with a sedentary lifestyle are prone to osteoporosis.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 230, "t": 7, "s": 11, "q": "The nurse provides menu options for a client prescribed a low-fat diet. Which of the following menu options should the nurse recommend to the client?", "o": ["Shrimp, avocado, and tomato salad.", "Liver, potato salad, and sherbet.", "Lean steak, mashed potatoes, and gravy.", "Turkey breast, boiled rice, and strawberries."], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: “Turkey breast, boiled rice, and strawberries” – Low fat options.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Shrimp, avocado, and tomato salad” – Avocado is high in fat.\n• “Liver, potato salad, and sherbet” – High in fat.\n• “Lean steak, mashed potatoes, and gravy” – High in fat.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 231, "t": 1, "s": 11, "q": "The charge nurse is re-assigning client rooms to accommodate new admissions. Which of the following clients should the nurse assign to the same room? Select all that apply.", "o": ["32-year-old female that is  2-day post op cholecystectomy and a 40-year-old female with pain due to pancreatitis.", "36-year-old female with diarrhea/vomiting of unknown origin and a 31-year-old female receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer.", "82-year-old male with pneumonia/candidiasis of the groin and a 76-year-old male with upper respiratory infection/thrush.", "46-year-old male with MRSA infection and a 50-year-old male with C.difficile  infection.", "18-year-old female that is 1-day post op appendectomy and an 84-year-old female who is agitated and confused."], "a": [0, 2], "r": "Correct:\n• “32-year-old female that is  2-day post op cholecystectomy and a 40-ye…” – Similar age, both post-op and nothing contagious.\n• “82-year-old male with pneumonia/candidiasis of the groin and a 76-yea…” – Similar age and both have respiratory infections and yeast.\n\nNot correct:\n• “36-year-old female with diarrhea/vomiting of unknown origin and a 31-…” – One client is immunocompromised and should be placed in a private room.\n• “46-year-old male with MRSA infection and a 50-year-old male with C.di…” – It is not good practice to place clients with different MDRO in the same room.\n• “18-year-old female that is 1-day post op appendectomy and an 84-year-…” – Different ages and safety risk for anyone roomed with an agitated and confused client.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 232, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client who asks the nurse to witness an advance directive. Which of the following actions should the nurse implement?", "o": ["Decline the client’s request politely.", "Agree to sign the document as a witness.", "Notify the PHCP of the client's request.", "Help the client locate an unrelated third party."], "a": 3, "r": "Witnesses to an advance directive should be impartial: not a relative, not an heir or beneficiary, and in many states not an employee of the care facility or the treating provider. The nurse supports the client’s autonomy by helping find an appropriate witness (an unrelated third party) rather than refusing or signing. State rules vary, so follow facility policy.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 233, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "The nurse needs to witness the signature on informed consent forms for multiple clients. Which of the following clients are legally able to sign a consent form for surgery? Select all that apply.", "o": ["16-year-old married client scheduled for a C-Section.", "59-year-old client who speaks Spanish only.", "30-year-old client who just received amoxicillin.", "88-year-old client who is blind but alert and oriented.", "17-year-old client who needs emergency appendectomy."], "a": [0, 2, 3], "r": "Correct:\n• 16-year-old married client – an emancipated minor (married under 18) can consent.\n• 30-year-old who received amoxicillin – an antibiotic does not impair the ability to give consent.\n• 88-year-old who is blind but alert and oriented – a disability that does not affect comprehension does not remove the right to sign.\n\nNot correct:\n• Spanish-only speaker – needs an interpreter before consent is informed.\n• 17-year-old needing an appendectomy – a minor who is not emancipated needs a parent or guardian (emergencies may proceed under implied consent).\n\n(From your class slides.)\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 234, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "The nurse is discussing living wills with a client. The client asks the nurse if a living will, prepared 3 years ago, remains in effect. Which response should the nurse provide to the client?", "o": ["“Yes, a living will never expire.”", "“You need to speak to an attorney.”", "“I will call someone to answer your question.”", "“It is recommended to review them yearly and update with your health care provider.”"], "a": 3, "r": "A living will stays valid until the client changes it, but it should be reviewed regularly (about yearly) and updated with the provider as health and wishes change. The nurse gives accurate teaching directly instead of deflecting to an attorney or another person.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 235, "t": 2, "s": 7, "q": "The nurse is teaching nursing staff about computerized documentation. Which of the following information should the nurse include?", "o": ["Document pain findings within the hour of assessment.", "Promote care continuity by using standardized care plans.", "Omit narrative progress notes for clinical condition changes.", "Use predefined statements for abnormal findings while charting by exception."], "a": 1, "r": "Electronic records support continuity of care through standardized care plans that every discipline can see. Charting by exception uses predefined statements for NORMAL findings, and abnormal findings and condition changes still require narrative notes, so the other options are wrong.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 236, "t": 7, "s": 12, "q": "The nurse in the outpatient clinic is talking with a 22-year-old client who is seeking treatment for constipation and given a prescription and discharge instructions for bisacodyl therapy. Which of the following statements by the client would require follow-up? Select all that apply.", "o": ["“I should drink 2 to 4 glasses of water per day.”", "“I will take the medication on an empty stomach to obtain quick relief from constipation.”", "“I will notify my health care provider if I should experience abdominal pain without any relief.”", "“I should make sure that I do not take the medication with an antacid even to prevent gastric upset.”", "“I should plan on taking the medication for the rest of my life to prevent any future episodes of discomfort.”"], "a": [0, 4], "r": "Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for short-term use. Follow-up is needed for: drinking only 2–4 glasses of water (the client should take in about 8–10 glasses a day to help the laxative work and prevent dehydration) and planning to use it for life (long-term use causes dependence and electrolyte loss). Taking it on an empty stomach, reporting unrelieved abdominal pain, and avoiding antacids (they dissolve the enteric coating early) are all correct.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 237, "t": 0, "s": 8, "q": "The nurse and unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) are caring for an agitated client who had physical restraints applied 5 minutes ago. Which of the following interventions would be appropriate for the nurse to assign to the UAP?", "o": ["Assessing the client’s behavior.", "Monitoring the client’s orientation level.", "Providing range-of-motion exercises for the client.", "Deciding whether or not to remove the client’s restraints."], "a": 2, "r": "UAPs may perform routine, stable tasks such as range of motion and basic care. Assessment, monitoring mental status, and the decision to remove restraints require the RN’s judgment and cannot be delegated.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 238, "t": 0, "s": 13, "q": "The nurse is caring for a group of assigned clients on the medical surgical unit. Which of the following clients should the nurse see first?", "o": ["Client with a spinal cord injury who is flushed, diaphoretic.", "Client with diabetes mellitus (type 2) who has a finger stick glucose level of 242 mg/dL.", "Client with hyperparathyroidism with a calcium level of 11.5 mg/dL.", "Client with angina complaining of a headache after receiving prescribed nitroglycerin."], "a": 0, "r": "A flushed, diaphoretic client with a spinal cord injury suggests autonomic dysreflexia, a life-threatening emergency (severe hypertension, risk of stroke). The glucose of 242, calcium of 11.5, and a nitroglycerin headache are expected or only mildly abnormal and can wait.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 239, "t": 0, "s": 13, "q": "The UAP reports that a client is complaining of chest pain. Which of the following actions should the nurse perform first?", "o": ["Ask the UAP to get a complete set of vital signs on the client.", "Call the rapid response team.", "Administer prescribed nitroglycerin sublingual tab.", "Assess the client."], "a": 3, "r": "Assessment comes first in the nursing process. The nurse must evaluate the chest pain before acting, then treat or escalate based on the findings.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 240, "t": 10, "s": 13, "q": "The nurse in the outpatient clinic is talking with a 42-year-old client who has hypertension and is receiving a loop diuretic. Which of the following statements by the client would require follow-up? Select all that apply.", "o": ["“I should still follow my low-sodium diet.”", "“I will stand slowly from a seated position.”", "“I will increase my fluid intake each day to 5L.”", "“I should weight myself once a month.”", "“I will take the medication at the same time of the day, every day.”"], "a": [2, 3], "r": "Requires follow-up: drinking 5 L a day (fluid should not be pushed excessively and should be guided by the provider) and weighing once a month (diuretic clients should weigh daily to track fluid loss or gain). Low-sodium diet, standing slowly to prevent orthostatic hypotension, and a consistent daily dosing time are correct.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 241, "t": 4, "s": 13, "q": "The nurse in the emergency department is caring for a 9-year-old client with asthma. Client reports chest tightness with cough especially at night for 2 evenings. Expiratory wheeze throughout the chest. T 98.6 °F, P 110, RR 28, SpO2 89% on room air. Sitting in tripod position. The nurse should first:", "o": ["apply humidified supplemental oxygen at 2L/min via nasal cannula", "ask parent about allergens in the home", "remind the client how to use metered dose inhaler", "check the client’s peak flow with a peak expiratory flow meter"], "a": 0, "r": "Airway, breathing, circulation: SpO2 of 89%, tachypnea, wheezing, and tripod positioning show hypoxemia and respiratory distress, so oxygen is first. Allergen history, inhaler teaching, and peak flow come after the client is stabilized (peak flow is also hard to do in acute distress).\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 242, "t": 10, "s": 13, "q": "The nurse in the outpatient clinic is caring for a 40-year-old client that is taking an ACE inhibitor. Which actions are indicated for this client? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Teach the client to report if a dry cough occurs", "Teach the client that the medication should be taken without food.", "Advise the client to increase potassium intake.", "Female clients who become pregnant should immediately notify the provider."], "a": [0, 3], "r": "ACE inhibitors commonly cause a dry cough (report it, the drug may need changing) and are teratogenic, so pregnancy must be reported at once. They cause potassium retention, so clients should NOT increase potassium intake, and most can be taken with or without food.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 243, "t": 16, "s": 13, "q": "A client is post-operative day 4 after an appendectomy. Which findings indicate the client is NOT progressing as expected? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Can ambulate in the corridor 2 times a day with minimal assistance.", "Jackson-Pratt drain has minimal drainage of 15 mL/day.", "Refusing to use the incentive spirometer because it causes too much pain.", "Using the client-controlled analgesia (PCA) device as ordered.", "States no nausea or vomiting.", "Abdominal incision dressing is clean, dry, and intact.", "Redness and edema noted at the incision site."], "a": [2, 6], "r": "Not progressing: refusing the incentive spirometer (risk of atelectasis and pneumonia, so pain control and teaching are needed) and redness and edema at the incision (possible early wound infection). The rest are expected recovery findings.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 244, "t": 5, "s": 9, "q": "A 72-year-old client on furosemide doubled the dose to catch up and now is drowsy, confused, weak, with numbness and tingling in the hands. ABG: pH 7.48, PaCO2 44 mm Hg, HCO3 30 mEq/L. Which acid-base imbalance is the client most likely experiencing?", "o": ["Metabolic acidosis.", "Respiratory acidosis.", "Metabolic alkalosis.", "Respiratory alkalosis."], "a": 2, "r": "pH 7.48 is alkalotic. The HCO3 of 30 is high (normal 22–28) and PaCO2 of 44 is normal, so the cause is metabolic. Excess loop diuretic loses H+, K+ and fluid, producing metabolic alkalosis.\n\n(From your class slides.)"}, {"id": 245, "t": 5, "s": 9, "q": "The client on furosemide has metabolic alkalosis after taking a doubled dose. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?", "o": ["Check the client’s skin turgor.", "Assess the client’s electrolyte value pattern.", "Ask the client if salt is added to their food at mealtimes.", "Have the client describe typical daily bowel and bladder habits."], "a": 1, "r": "Overuse of furosemide can cause hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, and hyponatremia, which explain the weakness, confusion, and tingling. Reviewing the electrolyte pattern directly identifies life-threatening imbalances first. Skin turgor and history questions are lower priority.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 246, "t": 11, "s": 10, "q": "A 26-year-old primigravida client at 4 weeks gestation arrives at the outpatient clinic for an initial prenatal visit. Which of the following findings would be consistent with the client’s history? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Fatigue.", "Nausea.", "Quickening.", "Breast sensitivity.", "Braxton Hicks contractions."], "a": [0, 1, 3], "r": "Early pregnancy (presumptive signs) includes fatigue, nausea, and breast tenderness. Quickening is first felt around 16–20 weeks (earlier in multiparas), and Braxton Hicks contractions appear in the second and third trimesters.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 247, "t": 12, "s": 14, "q": "The nurse in the pediatric clinic is caring for a 6-year-old client with recurrent group A streptococcal pharyngitis and inflammation of the conjunctivae. The eyelids are edematous. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?", "o": ["Moisten a clean compress with warm water.", "Remind the client to avoid rubbing their eyes.", "Instill newly prescribed antibiotic drops in both eyes.", "Wipe each client eye beginning at the inner canthus."], "a": 0, "r": "Crusted drainage must be softened and removed before medication can reach the eye, so the nurse first prepares a warm, moist compress. Then wipe from the inner to the outer canthus with a clean area of cloth for each eye, instill drops, and teach not to rub the eyes.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 248, "t": 14, "s": 11, "q": "The community health nurse is preparing to host a seminar about preventive care at the local church. Which of the following information should the nurse include for the seminar?", "o": ["Family planning and well-baby care.", "Adult screening for blood pressure and cholesterol.", "Family focused medical home and ambulatory care.", "Rehabilitative programs such as sports medicine and orthopedic."], "a": 0, "r": "Preventive care is the primary level of health care, which focuses on health promotion and protection such as family planning and well-baby care. Screening is secondary, and rehabilitation is tertiary.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 249, "t": 14, "s": 11, "q": "The charge nurse in the long-term-care facility is preparing to deliver an in-service about the secondary level of health care for staff nurses. Which of the following information should the nurse include in the seminar?", "o": ["Family planning and well-baby care.", "Adult screening for blood pressure and cholesterol.", "Family focused medical home and ambulatory care.", "Rehabilitative programs such as sports medicine and orthopedic."], "a": 1, "r": "Secondary prevention is screening and early diagnosis and treatment, such as adult blood pressure and cholesterol screening. Family planning and well-baby care are primary, and rehabilitation is tertiary.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 250, "t": 1, "s": 5, "q": "A nurse is working with an emergency response team caring for a group of people who may have been exposed to anthrax while doing farm work but are not exhibiting manifestations of illness. Select three prescriptions that the nurse would anticipate receiving from the primary health care provider (PHCP).", "o": ["Ciprofloxacin.", "Propranolol.", "Hydroxychloroquine.", "Furosemide.", "Doxycycline.", "Clindamycin."], "a": [0, 4, 5], "r": "Antibiotics are used for anthrax exposure. Ciprofloxacin and doxycycline are the first-line choices and clindamycin is also used in anthrax regimens. Propranolol, hydroxychloroquine, and furosemide do not treat anthrax. Confirm the exact regimen against your class notes.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 251, "t": 10, "s": 15, "q": "The home health nurse is caring for a 26-year-old client who has pulmonary tuberculosis and is receiving newly prescribed streptomycin 1 gram intramuscular (IM) once daily. Which of the following actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "o": ["Remind the client to monitor for hearing changes.", "Tell the client to contact the primary health care provider if the stool is black.", "Advise the client to take over-the-counter acetaminophen for fever and headache.", "Inform the client that there is no need for a follow-up visit once the medication is completed.", "Advise the client to continue streptomycin therapy before any procedures that may require anesthesia."], "a": [0, 2], "r": "Correct:\n• “Remind the client to monitor for hearing changes.” – Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside and can damage the 8th cranial nerve (hearing and balance).\n• “Advise the client to take over-the-counter acetaminophen…” – Acetaminophen is the safer choice for fever and headache; NSAIDs add kidney risk to an aminoglycoside.\n\nNot correct:\n• Black stool is not an expected effect of streptomycin.\n• Follow-up visits are still needed after treatment (TB therapy is long and monitored).\n• Streptomycin can increase the effect of anesthesia and muscle relaxants, so the client should tell the provider rather than simply continue it before procedures.\n\n(From your class slides: Respiratory, slide 27.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The acetaminophen option is the least certain part of this key."}, {"id": 252, "t": 7, "s": 16, "q": "The nurse is preparing to teach an in-service for nursing staff about post operative care for the client following abdominal surgery. Which content should the nurse speak about in the in-service? Select all that apply.", "o": ["“Therapeutic touch is an effective way to decrease postoperative abdominal pain.”", "“Total body relaxation is an effective adjunct for postoperative pain management.”", "“The nursing process is linear throughout the postoperative care of clients who have had abdominal surgery.”", "“The nurse’s priority is to check on the client who had abdominal surgery and returned to the unit just now and then check on the client who is requesting pain medication and returned from abdominal surgery 12 hours ago.”"], "a": [0, 1, 3], "r": "Correct:\n• Therapeutic touch and total body relaxation are accepted non-drug adjuncts for post-operative pain.\n• The newly returned post-operative client is seen first (airway, vital signs, bleeding) before the client at 12 hours who wants pain medication.\n\nNot correct: The nursing process is not linear; it is continuous and cyclical, so assessment and evaluation keep repeating.\n\n(From your class slides: GI, slide 32.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The therapeutic touch option is the least certain part of this key."}, {"id": 253, "t": 7, "s": 16, "q": "The nurse is preparing to teach an in-service for nursing staff and students about total parenteral nutrition. Which information should the nurse include in the in-service? Select all that apply.", "o": ["“Clients with paralytic ileus or severe diarrhea are candidates for parenteral nutrition.”", "“Total parenteral nutrition is delivered for the client through a gastrostomy or nasogastric tube.”", "“Protein, electrolyte, and glucose nutritional imbalances may be supplemented with total parenteral nutrition.”", "“Clients who need long-term nutritional supplementation will receive total parenteral nutrition via IV solutions comprised of 5% dextrose.”"], "a": [0, 2], "r": "Correct: TPN is for clients who cannot use the GI tract (for example paralytic ileus or severe diarrhea) and can supplement protein, electrolytes and glucose.\n\nNot correct:\n• Tube feeding through a gastrostomy or nasogastric tube is enteral nutrition; TPN is given by IV.\n• Long-term TPN is given through a central line in concentrated dextrose solutions; 5% dextrose supplies too few calories.\n\n(From your class slides: GI, slide 33.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 254, "t": 3, "s": 17, "q": "The nurse has taught a client who has hypertension and a body mass index of 28. Which of the following statements by the client would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching?", "o": ["“I should limit my sodium intake to 3300 mg per day.”", "“I will participate in a maximum of 1 hour of exercise per week.”", "“I can drink the maximum of 3 beverages that contain alcohol per day.”", "“I am going to eat less calories per day by following the Mediterranean diet.”"], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: A lower-calorie heart-healthy eating pattern such as the Mediterranean diet supports weight loss, which lowers blood pressure.\n\nNot correct:\n• Sodium should be limited to about 2 g a day, not 3300 mg (class slide 5).\n• Aerobic exercise should be increased; 1 hour a week is too little.\n• Alcohol should be limited; 3 drinks a day is too many.\n\n(From your class slides: Cardiac, slide 46.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 255, "t": 16, "s": 18, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client who has severe anemia and requires a transfusion of packed red blood cells (PRBCs). Which of the following four actions should the nurse plan to take?", "o": ["Check vital signs before transfusion.", "Insert an 18-gauge intravenous (IV) line.", "Prime the blood tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride.", "Transfuse the PRBCs within 6 hours.", "Check the expiration date of the PRBCs with a second nurse."], "a": [0, 1, 2, 4], "r": "Correct: Take baseline vital signs, use a large-bore (18-gauge) IV, prime the tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride only, and verify the blood product and expiration with a second licensed nurse.\n\nNot correct: A unit of PRBCs should be completed within 4 hours of leaving the blood bank; 6 hours is too long.\n\n(From your class slides: Hematology, slide 14.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 256, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "A client has Diabetes Insipidus. Which nursing intervention is essential for monitoring this condition?", "o": ["Inserting a Foley catheter", "Measuring intake and output", "Analyzing blood glucose levels", "Sending a urine specimen to the lab"], "a": 1, "r": "Correct: Diabetes insipidus causes very large volumes of dilute urine (2–20 L a day), so measuring intake and output is essential, along with daily weights.\n\nNot correct: A Foley is not routinely needed, and blood glucose is not the problem; diabetes insipidus is unrelated to diabetes mellitus.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 8.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 257, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client who has syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH).  Which of the following assessment findings would indicate that the client is developing a complication?", "o": ["A weight loss of 3 pounds in 4 days", "Observance of neck vein distention", "A decrease in blood pressure", "An increase in urine output"], "a": 1, "r": "Correct: In SIADH the body retains water, so neck vein distention is a sign of fluid overload.\n\nNot correct: Weight loss, lower blood pressure and increased urine output are not expected with water retention (SIADH gives low urine output, weight gain and low serum sodium).\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 9.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 258, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "The nurse is assessing a client who has possible Cushing’s syndrome.  The nurse expects to find:", "o": ["hypotension.", "thick, coarse skin.", "truncal obesity.", "weight gain in arms and legs."], "a": 2, "r": "Correct: Cushing's syndrome causes truncal obesity (with thin arms and legs), a rounded face, hypertension, muscle wasting and carbohydrate intolerance.\n\nNot correct: Hypotension is seen in Addison's disease; thick, coarse skin in hypothyroidism; and the extremities are thin, not heavy, in Cushing's.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 17.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 259, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "A nurse is educating on blood sugar control to the client who has uncontrolled diabetes that their vision may be impaired from:", "o": ["Prolonged periods of hyperglycemia", "Frequent injections of regular insulin", "Lens opacity", "Corneal dryness"], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: Prolonged hyperglycemia damages the small vessels of the retina (diabetic retinopathy) and can change the lens, impairing vision. Good glucose control protects the eyes.\n\nNot correct: Insulin injections do not cause vision problems; lens opacity (cataract) and corneal dryness are not the effect of high blood glucose being taught here.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 22.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 260, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "Hemoglobin A1C provides information on glucose control over the past:", "o": ["20 to 40 days", "50 to 65 days", "60 to 85 days", "90-120 days"], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: The hemoglobin A1C reflects average glucose control over about 90–120 days (roughly 3 months), the life span of red blood cells.\n\nNot correct: The shorter time frames are too short.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 23.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 261, "t": 6, "s": 19, "q": "The nurse is caring for a client who has pheochromocytoma.  Which of the following is the nurse most likely to assess?", "o": ["Constricted pupils", "Blood glucose level of 130 mg/dl", "Bradycardia", "Blood pressure of 176/88 mm Hg"], "a": 3, "r": "Correct: A pheochromocytoma releases excess catecholamines, causing hypertension (176/88 mm Hg), tachycardia, headache, sweating and anxiety.\n\nNot correct: Constricted pupils and bradycardia are the opposite effects, and a glucose of 130 mg/dL is not a key finding.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 25.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 262, "t": 10, "s": 19, "q": "The nurse is teaching a client who has diabetes mellitus (type 2) and a prescription for extended-release metformin. Select 2 statements the nurse should make.", "o": ["“You may chew the tablet if you experience any difficulty swallowing.”", "“You need to carry emergency identification and a glucagon kit at all times.”", "“You should contact the physician if you notice the metformin tablet in the stool.”", "“You will need to discontinue the medication for any procedure that requires a contrast agent.”"], "a": [1, 3], "r": "Correct:\n• “You will need to discontinue the medication for any procedure that requires a contrast agent.” – Metformin is held around contrast because of kidney injury and lactic acidosis risk.\n• “You need to carry emergency identification and a glucagon kit…” – the likely intended second answer for this select-two item.\n\nNot correct:\n• Extended-release tablets must be swallowed whole, never chewed.\n• The empty tablet shell can appear in the stool and is harmless; there is no need to call.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 26.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The glucagon-kit option is the least certain part of this key (metformin alone rarely causes low blood sugar)."}, {"id": 263, "t": 8, "s": 20, "q": "The nurse in the community clinic is caring for a 70-year-old client who has been wearing new bilateral hearing aids for 2 weeks. Which of the following should the nurse include in the client’s care plan?", "o": ["Check the client’s dexterity with hearing aid use.", "Communicate with the client above the client’s eye level.", "Speak with the client using loud, high-pitched tones.", "Remind the client to take off the glasses while wearing hearing aids."], "a": 0, "r": "Correct: A new hearing-aid user needs the nurse to check that the client can handle and adjust the devices (dexterity) and has realistic expectations.\n\nNot correct:\n• Speak at eye level, face to face, not above it.\n• Use a normal, lower-pitched tone; loud, high-pitched speech is harder to understand and can be distorted.\n• Glasses can be worn with hearing aids, and the client does not need to remove them.\n\n(From your class slides: Neurological, slide 80.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"}, {"id": 264, "t": 8, "s": 21, "q": "The nurse has taught a client who has sustained a right tibial fracture and had an external fixation device applied 36 hours ago. Which of the following statements by the client would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching? Select all that apply.", "o": ["“I will use chlorhexidine to clean around the pins.”", "“I should limit my daily consumption of foods high in vitamin D.”", "“I should expect to see some redness around the external pins.”", "“I know that the metal rods help steady my leg fracture as it is healing.”", "“I will notify my doctor if I experience increased pressure to the point of pain around my heel.”"], "a": [0, 3, 4], "r": "Correct:\n• Pin sites are cleaned with an antiseptic such as chlorhexidine, using aseptic technique.\n• The metal frame holds the fracture steady while it heals.\n• Increasing pressure and pain, for example around the heel, may signal compartment syndrome and must be reported.\n\nNot correct:\n• Vitamin D (and calcium) helps bone healing, so it should not be limited.\n• Redness at the pin sites should be reported as a possible infection rather than accepted as normal.\n\n(From your class slides: Musculoskeletal, slide 22.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The pin-site redness item is the least certain part of this key."}]};